window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-20"]=[{"id":"q3-44","q":"3:44","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:44 · Unseen reports and four kinds of revelation","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 352–353","cairo":"I:352–353","source_page_first":352,"source_page_last":353,"ar":"ذلِكَ مِنْ أَنْباءِ الْغَيْبِ نُوحِيهِ إِلَيْكَ وَما كُنْتَ لَدَيْهِمْ إِذْ يُلْقُونَ أَقْلامَهُمْ أَيُّهُمْ يَكْفُلُ مَرْيَمَ وَما كُنْتَ لَدَيْهِمْ إِذْ يَخْتَصِمُونَ (44)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله، لحبيبه صلّى الله عليه وسلم: ذلِكَ القصص الذي أطلعتك عليه، هو مِنْ أخبار الْغَيْبِ الذي لم يكن لك به شعور، وما عرفته إلا بوحينا وإعلامنا، فلا يشك في نبوتك إلا مطموس أعمى، (و) أيضاً: ما\nكُنْتَ لَدَيْهِمْ\nأي: عندهم، حين كانوا يُلْقُونَ أَقْلامَهُمْ لما اقترعوا، أَيُّهُمْ يَكْفُلُ مَرْيَمَ، وَما كُنْتَ لَدَيْهِمْ إِذْ يَخْتَصِمُونَ في كفالتها، فتخبرهم عما شهدت، بل لم يكن شيء من ذلك، فتعين أن يكون وحيا حقيقا، لأنه عليه الصلاة والسلام- كان أمياً لم يطالع شيئاً من كتب الأخبار، ولا جلس إلى من طالعهم من الأحبار، بإجماع الخاص والعام. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن الوحي على أربعة أقسام: وحي منام، ووحي إلهام، ووحي أحكام، ووحي إعلام، وشاركت الأولياءُ الأنبياءَ في ثلاثة: الإلهام والمنام والإعلام، إن كان بغير الملَك، ومعنى وحي إعلام: هو إطلاع الله النبيّ على أمور مغيبة، فإن كان بواسطة الملك، فهو مختص بالأنبياء، كما اختصت بوحي الأحكام، وأما إن كان بالإلهام أو بالمنام أو بالفهم عن الله، فيكون أيضاً للأولياء، إذ الروح إذا تصفت وتطهرت من دنس الحس أطلعها الله على غيبه في الجملة، وأما التفصيل فلا يعلمه إلا علاّم الغيوب. والله أعلم.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى البشارة بعيسى عليه السلام، وهو المقصود الأعظم من هذه القصص ليتخلص للرد على النصارى فى زعمهم الفاسد فيه، فقال:","quran_ar":"ذلِكَ مِنْ أَنْباءِ الْغَيْبِ نُوحِيهِ إِلَيْكَ وَما كُنْتَ لَدَيْهِمْ إِذْ يُلْقُونَ أَقْلامَهُمْ أَيُّهُمْ يَكْفُلُ مَرْيَمَ وَما كُنْتَ لَدَيْهِمْ إِذْ يَخْتَصِمُونَ (44)","quran_en":"44. That is among the tidings of the unseen which We reveal to you. You were not with them when they cast their pens [to decide] which of them would take charge of Mary, nor were you with them when they disputed.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The story as a sign of prophecy","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says to His beloved, blessings and peace be upon him: “That” account, which I have enabled you to know, belongs to “the tidings of the unseen” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أنباء الغيب<\/bdi>), of which you had no previous awareness and which you knew only through Our revelation and communication. None therefore doubts your prophecy except one whose inward vision has been utterly extinguished. “You were not with them”—present among them—when they “cast their pens” to draw lots for “which of them would take charge of Mary.” “Nor were you with them when they disputed” over her guardianship, so as to have reported something you witnessed. None of that happened. It must therefore have been a true revelation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وحي<\/bdi>), since the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, was unlettered (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أميّ<\/bdi>): he had not studied historical books or sat with scholars acquainted with them, as both the learned and the public agreed. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says to His beloved, blessings and peace be upon him: “That” account, which I have enabled you to know, belongs to “the tidings of the unseen” ( أنباء الغيب ), of which you had no previous awareness and which you knew only through Our revelation and communication. None therefore doubts your prophecy except one whose inward vision has been utterly extinguished. “You were not with them”—present among them—when they “cast their pens” to draw lots for “which of them would take charge of Mary.” “Nor were you with them when they disputed” over her guardianship, so as to have reported something you witnessed. None of that happened. It must therefore have been a true revelation ( وحي ), since the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, was unlettered ( أميّ ): he had not studied historical books or sat with scholars acquainted with them, as both the learned and the public agreed. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Dream, inspiration, legislation and disclosure","html":"<p>Know that <i>waḥy<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوحي<\/bdi>) is of four kinds: revelation in a dream (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وحي منام<\/bdi>), revelatory inspiration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وحي إلهام<\/bdi>), revelation of rulings (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وحي أحكام<\/bdi>), and revelation conveying information (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وحي إعلام<\/bdi>). God’s friends share with the prophets in three of them: inspiration, dreams, and disclosure of information—provided that the last does not come through an angel. The meaning of <i>waḥy al-iʿlām<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وحي إعلام<\/bdi>) is God’s acquainting a prophet with concealed matters. If it comes through an angel it belongs especially to prophets, just as revelation of rulings belongs especially to them. If, however, it comes by inspiration, dream or direct understanding from God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفهم عن الله<\/bdi>), it can also occur to God’s friends: once the spirit is clarified and cleansed of sensory defilement, God may disclose something of the unseen to it in a general way. Its particulars, however, none knows except the Knower of unseen things. And God knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N013\">[XIV-N013]<\/a><\/p><p>The Real then mentions the good news concerning Jesus, upon him peace—the chief purpose of this narrative, since it prepares the rebuttal to the Christians’ mistaken claim about him—saying:<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that waḥy ( الوحي ) is of four kinds: revelation in a dream ( وحي منام ), revelatory inspiration ( وحي إلهام ), revelation of rulings ( وحي أحكام ), and revelation conveying information ( وحي إعلام ). God’s friends share with the prophets in three of them: inspiration, dreams, and disclosure of information—provided that the last does not come through an angel. The meaning of waḥy al-iʿlām ( وحي إعلام ) is God’s acquainting a prophet with concealed matters. If it comes through an angel it belongs especially to prophets, just as revelation of rulings belongs especially to them. If, however, it comes by inspiration, dream or direct understanding from God ( الفهم عن الله ), it can also occur to God’s friends: once the spirit is clarified and cleansed of sensory defilement, God may disclose something of the unseen to it in a general way. Its particulars, however, none knows except the Knower of unseen things. And God knows best. [XIV-N013] The Real then mentions the good news concerning Jesus, upon him peace—the chief purpose of this narrative, since it prepares the rebuttal to the Christians’ mistaken claim about him—saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2093,"arabic_characters":1354},{"id":"q3-45-51","q":"3:45–51","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:45–51 · The annunciation, miracles and mission of Jesus","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 353–357","cairo":"I:353–357","source_page_first":353,"source_page_last":357,"ar":"إِذْ قالَتِ الْمَلائِكَةُ يَا مَرْيَمُ إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُبَشِّرُكِ بِكَلِمَةٍ مِنْهُ اسْمُهُ الْمَسِيحُ عِيسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ وَجِيهاً فِي الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ وَمِنَ الْمُقَرَّبِينَ (45) وَيُكَلِّمُ النَّاسَ فِي الْمَهْدِ وَكَهْلاً وَمِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ (46) قالَتْ رَبِّ أَنَّى يَكُونُ لِي وَلَدٌ وَلَمْ يَمْسَسْنِي بَشَرٌ قالَ كَذلِكِ اللَّهُ يَخْلُقُ ما يَشاءُ إِذا قَضى أَمْراً فَإِنَّما يَقُولُ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ (47) وَيُعَلِّمُهُ الْكِتابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَالتَّوْراةَ وَالْإِنْجِيلَ (48) وَرَسُولاً إِلى بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ أَنِّي قَدْ جِئْتُكُمْ بِآيَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ أَنِّي أَخْلُقُ لَكُمْ مِنَ الطِّينِ كَهَيْئَةِ الطَّيْرِ فَأَنْفُخُ فِيهِ فَيَكُونُ طَيْراً بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ وَأُبْرِئُ الْأَكْمَهَ وَالْأَبْرَصَ وَأُحْيِ الْمَوْتى بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ وَأُنَبِّئُكُمْ بِما تَأْكُلُونَ وَما تَدَّخِرُونَ فِي بُيُوتِكُمْ إِنَّ فِي ذلِكَ لَآيَةً لَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (49)\nوَمُصَدِّقاً لِما بَيْنَ يَدَيَّ مِنَ التَّوْراةِ وَلِأُحِلَّ لَكُمْ بَعْضَ الَّذِي حُرِّمَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَجِئْتُكُمْ بِآيَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَأَطِيعُونِ (50) إِنَّ اللَّهَ رَبِّي وَرَبُّكُمْ فَاعْبُدُوهُ هذا صِراطٌ مُسْتَقِيمٌ (51)\nقلت: (إذ قالت) : بدل من (وإذ قالت) الأولى، ويبعد إبدالها من (إذ يختصمون) ، و (المسيح) وما بعده: أخبار عن اسمه، أو (عيسى) : خبر عن مضمر، و (ابن مريم) : صفته، و (المسيح) : فعيل بمعنى مفعول، لأنه مُسِحَ من الأقذار، أي: طهر منها، أو مسح بالبركة، أو كان مسيح القدم، لا أخمص له، أو مسحه جبريل بجناحه من الشيطان. أو بمعنى فاعل لأنه كان يمسح المرضى فيبرءون، أو يمسح عين الأعمى فيبصر، أو لأنه كان يسيح في الأرض ولا يقيم في مكان فتكون الميم زائدة.\nوأما المسيح الدجّال فإنه ممسوح إحدى العينين، أو لأنه يطوف الأرض ويمسحها، إلا مكة والمدينة، والحاصل:\nأن عيسى مسيح الخير، والدجال مسيح الشر، ولذلك قيل: إن المسيح يقتل المسيح. و (وجيهاً) : حال من (كلمة) لتخصيصه بالصفة، و (في المهد وكهلاً) : حالان، أي: طفلاً وكهلاً، والمهد: ما يمهد للصبي. و (رسولاً) : مفعولٌ لمحذوف، أي: ونجعله رسولاً، و (مصدقاً) : عطف على (رسولاً) ، و (لأُحِلَّ) : متعلق بمحذوف، أي: وجئتكم لأُحل.\nأو معطوف على معنى مصدقاً، كقولهم: جئتك معتذراً، أو لأطيب قلبك.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: (و) اذكر أيضا إِذْ قالَتِ الْمَلائِكَةُ في بشارتهم لمريم: يا مَرْيَمُ إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُبَشِّرُكِ بِكَلِمَةٍ مِنْهُ، أي: بولد يتكوَّن بكلمة من الله كن فيكون، وقيل: إنما سمى كلمة لكونه مظهراً لكلمة التكوين، متحققاً ومتصرفاً بها. ولذلك كان يظهر عليه خوارق الأقدار أكثر من غيره من الأنبياء، اسْمُهُ الْمَسِيحُ، واسمه عِيسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ، وإنما قال: ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ والخطاب لها، تنبيهاً على أنه يولد من غير أب إذ الأولاد إنما تنسب لأبائها إلا إذا فقد الأب. ثم وصف الولد بقوله: وَجِيهاً فِي الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ أي: شريفاً في الدنيا بالنبوة والرسالة، وفي الآخرة بالشفاعة لمن تبعه. ويكون مِنَ الْمُقَرَّبِينَ إلى الله تعالى في الدارين.\nوَيُكَلِّمُ النَّاسَ طفلاً فِي الْمَهْدِ على وجه خَرْق العادة في تبرئة أمه، وَكَهْلًا إذا كمل عقله قبل أن يرفع، أو بعد الرفع والنزول، لأن الكهولة بعد الأربعين، والتحقيق: أنه بشرها بنبوة عيسى وكلامه في المهد، معجزةً، وفي الكهولة دعوة قبل الرفع وبعده، وما قارب الشيء يُعطي حكمه، وحال كونه مِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ لحضرة رب العالمين.\nولما سمعت البشارة دهشت وقالَتْ: يا رَبِّ أَنَّى يَكُونُ لِي وَلَدٌ وَلَمْ يَمْسَسْنِي بَشَرٌ، والخطاب لله، فانية عن الواسطة جبريل، والاستفهام تعجباً، أو عن الكيفية: هل يكون بتزوج أم لا؟ قالَ لها الملك: كَذلِكِ اللَّهُ يَخْلُقُ ما يَشاءُ. أو الأمر كذلك كما تقولين، لكن اللَّهُ يَخْلُقُ ما يَشاءُ لا يحتاج إلى وسائط ولا أسباب، بل إِذا قَضى أَمْراً فَإِنَّما يَقُولُ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ، وَيُعَلِّمُهُ الْكِتابَ أي: الكتابة والخط، وَالْحِكْمَةَ أي:\nالنبوة، أو الإصابة في الرأي، وَالتَّوْراةَ وَالْإِنْجِيلَ.\n(و) يجعله رَسُولًا إِلى بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ. وكان أول رسل بني إسرائيل يوسف، وآخرهم عيسى- عليهما السلام-، وقال: عليه الصلاة والسلام: «بُعثْتُ على إِثْرِ ثمانية آلاف نبيّ، أربعة آلاف من بني إسرائيل» . فإذا بعث إليهم قال: أَنِّي قَدْ جِئْتُكُمْ بِآيَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ أي: بأني قَدْ جِئْتُكُمْ بِآيَةٍ مِّن ربكم، قالوا: وما هي؟ قال:\nأَنِّي أَخْلُقُ لَكُمْ مِنَ الطِّينِ كَهَيْئَةِ الطَّيْرِ كصورته، فَأَنْفُخُ فِيهِ فَيَكُونُ طَيْراً بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ، وكان يخلق لهم صورة الخفاش، لأنها أكمل الطير لأن لها ثدياً وأسناناً وتحيض وتطير، فيكون أبلغ في المعجزة، وكان يطير مادام الناس ينظرون إليه، فإذا غاب عنهم سقط ميتاً ليتميز فعل الحق من فعل الخلق.\nثم قال لهم: ولي معجزة أخرى أني أُبْرِئُ الْأَكْمَهَ الذي ولد أعمى، فأحرى غيره، وَالْأَبْرَصَ الذي فيه وضح(1) . وخصهما لأنهما عاهتان معضلتان. وكان الغالب في زمن عيسى الطب، فأراهم المعجزة من جنس ذلك. رُوِي: أنه ربما اجتمع عليه من المرضى في اليوم الواحد ألوف، من أطاق منهم البلوغ(2) أتاه، ومن لم يطق أتاه عيسى عليه السلام، وإنما كان يداويهم بالدعاء على شرط الإسلام.\nوَأُحْيِ الْمَوْتى بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ لا بقدرتي دفعاً لتوهم الألوهية، فإن الإحياء ليس من طوق البشر. رُوِيَ أنه أحيا أربعة أنفس: (العازر) ، وكان صديقاً له، فأرسلت أخته إلى عيسى أن أخاك العازر يموت، فأتاه من مسيرة ثلاثة أيام فوجده مات، فقال لأخته: انطلقي بنا إلى قبره، وهو في صخرة مطبقة، فدعا الله تعالى، فقام العازر يقطر ودكه(3) ، فعاش وولد له. و (ابن العجوز) ، مُر بجنازته على عيسى عليه السلام فدعا الله تعالى، فجلس على سريره، ونزل عن أعناق الرجال، ولبس ثيابه، وحمل سريره على عنقه، ورجع إلى أهله، وبقي حتى وُلد له. و (ابنة العاشر) ، كان يأخذ العشور، قيل له: أتحييها، وقد ماتت أمس؟ فدعا الله تعالى، فعاشت وولد لها. و (سام بن نوح) ، دعا باسم الله الأعظم، فخرج من قبره، وقد شاب نصف رأسه، فقال: أقامت الساعة؟ قال: لا، لكني دعوت الله فأحياك، مالى أرى الشيب في رأسك، ولم يكن في زمانك؟ قال: سمعت الصيحة، فظننت أن الساعة قامت فشبت من هولها. قيل: كان يحيي الموتى ب يا حيّ يا قيوم.\nوَأُنَبِّئُكُمْ بِما تَأْكُلُونَ وَما تَدَّخِرُونَ فِي بُيُوتِكُمْ، لما أبرأ الأكمه والأبرص قالوا: هذا سحر، أخبرنا بما نأكل وما ندخر؟ فكان يُخبر الرجل بما يأكل في غدائه وعشائه. ورُوِيَ أنه لمَّا كان في المكتب، كان يحدث الغلمان بما يصلح لهم آباؤهم من الطعام، فيقول للغلام: انطلق ... غداء أهلك كذا وكذا، فيقول أهله: من أخبرك بهذا؟ قال: عيسى، فحبسوا صبيانهم عنه، وقالوا: لا تلعبوا مع هذا الساحر، فجمعوهم في بيت، فجاء عيسى\nيطلبهم، فقالوا: ليسوا هاهنا، قال: ماذا في البيت؟ قالوا: خنازير، قال عيسى: كذلك يكونون، ففتحوا الباب، فإذاهم خنازير، فهموا بقتله، فهربت به أمه إلى مصر. قاله السُّدي.\nثم قال لهم: إِنَّ فِي ذلِكَ لَآيَةً لَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ، فإن غير المؤمنين لا ينتفع بالمعجزات لعناده، وَمُصَدِّقاً لِما بَيْنَ يَدَيَّ مِنَ التَّوْراةِ أي: وجئتكم مصدقاً للتوراة، وشاهداً على صحتها، وَلِأُحِلَّ لَكُمْ بَعْضَ الَّذِي حُرِّمَ عَلَيْكُمْ في شريعة موسى عليه السلام كالشحوم والثروب(1) ولحم الإبل والعمل في السبت. وهذا يدل على أنه ناسخ للتوراة، ولا يخل بكونه مصدقاً له، كما لا يخل نسخ القرآن بعضه لبعض بصحته. فإن النسخ في الحقيقة: بيان لانتهاء العمل بذلك الحكم. ثم قال لهم: (و) قد جِئْتُكُمْ بِآيَةٍ واضحة مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ، قد شاهدتموها بأعينكم، فما بقي إلا عنادكم، فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَأَطِيعُونِ.\nثم دعاهم إلى التوحيد بعد بيان الحجة فقال: إِنَّ اللَّهَ رَبِّي وَرَبُّكُمْ فَاعْبُدُوهُ ولا تعبدوا معه سواه، هذا صِراطٌ مُسْتَقِيمٌ لا عوج فيه. قال البيضاوي: أي: لما جئتكم بالمعجزات القاهرة والآيات الباهرة، فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ في المخالفة، وَأَطِيعُونِ فيما أدعوكم إليه، ثم شرع في الدعوة وأشار إليها بالقول المجمل، فقال: إِنَّ اللَّهَ رَبِّي وَرَبُّكُمْ أشار إلى استكمال القوة النظرية بالاعتقاد الحق الذي غايتُه التوحيد، وقال: فَاعْبُدُوهُ إشارة إلى استكمال القوة العملية بملازمة الطاعة، التي هي الإتيان بالأوامر والانتهاء عن المناهي، ثم قرر ذلك بأن الجمع بين الأمرين هو الطريق المشهود له بالاستقامة، ونظيره: قوله عليه الصلاة والسلام: «قُلْ آمَنْتُ بِاللهِ ثم اسْتَقِمْ» .\nالإشارة: كل من انقطع بكليته إلى مولاه، وصدف عن حظوظه وهواه، وأفنى شبابه في طاعة ربه، وجعل يلتمس في حياته دواء قلبه، تحققت له البشارة في العاجل والآجل، وحصل له التطهير من درن العيوب والرذائل، ورزقه من فواكه العلوم، ما تتضاءل دون إدراكه غاية الفهوم، هذه مريم البتول أفنت شبابها في طاعة مولاها، فقربها إليه وتولاها، وبشرها بالاصطفائية والتطهير، وأمرها شكراً بالجد والتشمير، ثم بشّرها ثانياً بالولد النزيه والسيد النبيه، روح الله وكلمة الله، من غير أب ولا سبب، ولا معالجة ولا تعب، أمره بأمر الله، يبرئ الأكمه والأبرص ويحيي الموتى بإذن الله، هذا كله ببركة الانقطاع وسر الاتباع.\nقال صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «من انقطع إلى الله كَفَاه الله كلَّ مُؤْنة، ورَزقه من حيثُ لا يحتسب، ومن انقطع إلى الدنيا وكَلَه الله تعالى إليها» .\nوقال بعضهم: صِدْقُ المجاهدة: الانقطاع إليه من كل شيء سواه. فالانقطاع إلى الله في الصغر يخدم على الإنسان في حال الكبر، ومعاصي الصغر تجر الوبال إلى الكبر، فكما أن عيسى عليه السلام كان يبرئ الأكمه والأبرص بإذن الله، كذلك من انقطع بكُلِّيته إلى الله أبرأ القلوبَ السقيمة بإذن الله، وأحيا موتى القلوب بذكر الله، وأخبر بالغيوب وما تدخره ضمائر القلوب، يدل على طاعة الله، ويدعو بحاله ومقاله إلى الله، يهدي الناس إلى الصراط المستقيم، ويوصل من اتبعه إلى حضرة النعيم. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nولما تمت البشارة بعيسى عليه السلام وظهر إلى الدنيا، وبلغ وقت الدعوة، بعثه الله إلى بنى إسرائيل، فكفروا به، فلما تحقق كفرهم طلب من ينصره إلى الله، كما أشار الحق تعالى إلى ذلك بقوله:","quran_ar":"إِذْ قالَتِ الْمَلائِكَةُ يَا مَرْيَمُ إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُبَشِّرُكِ بِكَلِمَةٍ مِنْهُ اسْمُهُ الْمَسِيحُ عِيسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ وَجِيهاً فِي الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ وَمِنَ الْمُقَرَّبِينَ (45) وَيُكَلِّمُ النَّاسَ فِي الْمَهْدِ وَكَهْلاً وَمِنَ الصَّالِحِينَ (46) قالَتْ رَبِّ أَنَّى يَكُونُ لِي وَلَدٌ وَلَمْ يَمْسَسْنِي بَشَرٌ قالَ كَذلِكِ اللَّهُ يَخْلُقُ ما يَشاءُ إِذا قَضى أَمْراً فَإِنَّما يَقُولُ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ (47) وَيُعَلِّمُهُ الْكِتابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَالتَّوْراةَ وَالْإِنْجِيلَ (48) وَرَسُولاً إِلى بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ أَنِّي قَدْ جِئْتُكُمْ بِآيَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ أَنِّي أَخْلُقُ لَكُمْ مِنَ الطِّينِ كَهَيْئَةِ الطَّيْرِ فَأَنْفُخُ فِيهِ فَيَكُونُ طَيْراً بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ وَأُبْرِئُ الْأَكْمَهَ وَالْأَبْرَصَ وَأُحْيِ الْمَوْتى بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ وَأُنَبِّئُكُمْ بِما تَأْكُلُونَ وَما تَدَّخِرُونَ فِي بُيُوتِكُمْ إِنَّ فِي ذلِكَ لَآيَةً لَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (49) وَمُصَدِّقاً لِما بَيْنَ يَدَيَّ مِنَ التَّوْراةِ وَلِأُحِلَّ لَكُمْ بَعْضَ الَّذِي حُرِّمَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَجِئْتُكُمْ بِآيَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَأَطِيعُونِ (50) إِنَّ اللَّهَ رَبِّي وَرَبُّكُمْ فَاعْبُدُوهُ هذا صِراطٌ مُسْتَقِيمٌ (51)","quran_en":"45. When the angels said, “Mary, God gives you glad tidings of a Word from Him, whose name is the Messiah, Jesus son of Mary, distinguished in this world and the Hereafter, and one of those brought near. 46. He shall speak to people in the cradle and in maturity, and shall be among the righteous.” 47. She said, “My Lord, how shall I have a son when no man has touched me?” He said, “Thus does God create whatever He wills. When He decrees a thing, He only says to it ‘Be!’ and it is.” 48. He will teach him the Book and Wisdom, the Torah and the Gospel. 49. And [He will make him] a messenger to the Children of Israel [saying], “I have come to you with a sign from your Lord: I fashion for you from clay something in the shape of a bird, breathe into it, and it becomes a bird by God’s leave. I heal the one born blind and the leper, and bring the dead to life by God’s leave. I tell you what you eat and what you store in your houses. Surely in this is a sign for you, if you are believers. 50. And I confirm what came before me of the Torah, and [have come] to make lawful for you some of what was forbidden to you. I have come to you with a sign from your Lord; so be mindful of God and obey me. 51. God is my Lord and your Lord; therefore worship Him. This is a straight path.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The Messiah, the Word, and the syntax of the annunciation","html":"<p>I say: “When the angels said” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إذ قالت<\/bdi>) is a substitute (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل<\/bdi>) for the earlier “when they said.” Taking it as a substitute for “when they disputed” is remote. “The Messiah” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المسيح<\/bdi>), and what follows, gives information about the child’s name; alternatively “Jesus” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عيسى<\/bdi>) is the predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>) of an omitted subject, “son of Mary” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ابن مريم<\/bdi>) being its attribute (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صفة<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>“Messiah” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المسيح<\/bdi>) follows the pattern <i>faʿīl<\/i> with a passive sense (<i>mafʿūl<\/i>): he was wiped or cleansed of impurities and so purified; or he was anointed with blessing; or the sole of his foot was flat without an arch; or Gabriel wiped him with his wing to protect him from Satan. Or <i>masīḥ<\/i> has an active sense (<i>fāʿil<\/i>), because he passed his hand over the ill and they recovered, or over the blind person’s eyes and they saw; or because he travelled (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يسِيح<\/bdi>) through the earth, without settling in one place, in which case the initial mīm is additional. As for the Antichrist, <i>al-masīḥ al-dajjāl<\/i>, one of his eyes is effaced, or he traverses the earth save Mecca and Medina. In short, Jesus is the Messiah of good and the Antichrist the messiah of evil. It is therefore said, “The Messiah will slay the messiah.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N014\">[XIV-N014]<\/a><\/p><p>“Distinguished” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وجيهًا<\/bdi>) is an accusative of state (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>) modifying “Word,” which has been sufficiently specified by its qualification. “In the cradle” and “in maturity” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في المهد وكهلًا<\/bdi>) are two circumstantial expressions: as an infant and as a mature man. The cradle (<i>mahd<\/i>) is the bedding prepared for an infant. “A messenger” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رسولًا<\/bdi>) is the object of an omitted verb: “And We shall make him a messenger.” “Confirming” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدقًا<\/bdi>) is coordinated (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عطف<\/bdi>) with “a messenger.” “So that I may make lawful” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ولأحل<\/bdi>) depends upon an omitted verb, “I have come to make lawful,” or is coordinated with the implied meaning of “confirming,” as in the idiom “I came to you apologizing—or to please your heart.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “When the angels said” ( إذ قالت ) is a substitute ( بدل ) for the earlier “when they said.” Taking it as a substitute for “when they disputed” is remote. “The Messiah” ( المسيح ), and what follows, gives information about the child’s name; alternatively “Jesus” ( عيسى ) is the predicate ( خبر ) of an omitted subject, “son of Mary” ( ابن مريم ) being its attribute ( صفة ). “Messiah” ( المسيح ) follows the pattern faʿīl with a passive sense ( mafʿūl ): he was wiped or cleansed of impurities and so purified; or he was anointed with blessing; or the sole of his foot was flat without an arch; or Gabriel wiped him with his wing to protect him from Satan. Or masīḥ has an active sense ( fāʿil ), because he passed his hand over the ill and they recovered, or over the blind person’s eyes and they saw; or because he travelled ( يسِيح ) through the earth, without settling in one place, in which case the initial mīm is additional. As for the Antichrist, al-masīḥ al-dajjāl , one of his eyes is effaced, or he traverses the earth save Mecca and Medina. In short, Jesus is the Messiah of good and the Antichrist the messiah of evil. It is therefore said, “The Messiah will slay the messiah.” [XIV-N014] “Distinguished” ( وجيهًا ) is an accusative of state ( حال ) modifying “Word,” which has been sufficiently specified by its qualification. “In the cradle” and “in maturity” ( في المهد وكهلًا ) are two circumstantial expressions: as an infant and as a mature man. The cradle ( mahd ) is the bedding prepared for an infant. “A messenger” ( رسولًا ) is the object of an omitted verb: “And We shall make him a messenger.” “Confirming” ( مصدقًا ) is coordinated ( عطف ) with “a messenger.” “So that I may make lawful” ( ولأحل ) depends upon an omitted verb, “I have come to make lawful,” or is coordinated with the implied meaning of “confirming,” as in the idiom “I came to you apologizing—or to please your heart.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The Word, speech from the cradle, and the divine creative command","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Remember when the angels said” in giving Mary good news, “Mary, God gives you glad tidings of a Word from Him”—a son formed by God’s Word “Be!” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كُنْ<\/bdi>) and thus brought into being. Some say he was called a “Word” because he manifested the creative Word (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كلمة التكوين<\/bdi>), realized it, and exercised effect through it. That is why extraordinary events appeared at his hands more often than at those of other prophets. “His name is the Messiah, Jesus son of Mary.” Although Mary herself is being addressed, he is called “son of Mary” to signal that he will be born without a father: children are ordinarily attributed to their fathers, except when the father is absent.<\/p><p>“Distinguished in this world and the Hereafter”: noble in this world through prophecy and messengership, and in the Hereafter through intercession for his followers. “And one of those brought near” to God in both abodes.<\/p><p>“He will speak to people in the cradle,” as an event that breaches the customary order (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خرق العادة<\/bdi>), vindicating his mother; “and in maturity,” after his understanding has become complete, before his being raised, or after his return, since maturity is after forty. The sound interpretation is that she was given tidings of Jesus’ prophecy and his speech in the cradle as a miracle, and of his mission of calling people in maturity before and after his being raised. What closely approaches a state may be treated under its ruling. He is “among the righteous”—those fit for the Presence of the Lord of the worlds.<\/p><p>When Mary heard the good news, she was astonished. “She said, My Lord, how shall I have a son when no man has touched me?” She addressed God, having become absent from awareness of the intermediary Gabriel (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فانية عن الواسطة<\/bdi>), whether in wonder or inquiring how: through marriage or without it? The angel said, “Thus does God create whatever He wills”; or: “The matter is as you say, yet God creates whatever He wills.” He needs neither intermediaries nor causes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوسائط والأسباب<\/bdi>). “When He decrees a thing, He merely says to it ‘Be!’ and it is.” He will teach him “the Book”—writing and the script—“and Wisdom”—prophecy or sound judgment—“and the Torah and the Gospel.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Remember when the angels said” in giving Mary good news, “Mary, God gives you glad tidings of a Word from Him”—a son formed by God’s Word “Be!” ( كُنْ ) and thus brought into being. Some say he was called a “Word” because he manifested the creative Word ( كلمة التكوين ), realized it, and exercised effect through it. That is why extraordinary events appeared at his hands more often than at those of other prophets. “His name is the Messiah, Jesus son of Mary.” Although Mary herself is being addressed, he is called “son of Mary” to signal that he will be born without a father: children are ordinarily attributed to their fathers, except when the father is absent. “Distinguished in this world and the Hereafter”: noble in this world through prophecy and messengership, and in the Hereafter through intercession for his followers. “And one of those brought near” to God in both abodes. “He will speak to people in the cradle,” as an event that breaches the customary order ( خرق العادة ), vindicating his mother; “and in maturity,” after his understanding has become complete, before his being raised, or after his return, since maturity is after forty. The sound interpretation is that she was given tidings of Jesus’ prophecy and his speech in the cradle as a miracle, and of his mission of calling people in maturity before and after his being raised. What closely approaches a state may be treated under its ruling. He is “among the righteous”—those fit for the Presence of the Lord of the worlds. When Mary heard the good news, she was astonished. “She said, My Lord, how shall I have a son when no man has touched me?” She addressed God, having become absent from awareness of the intermediary Gabriel ( فانية عن الواسطة ), whether in wonder or inquiring how: through marriage or without it? The angel said, “Thus does God create whatever He wills”; or: “The matter is as you say, yet God creates whatever He wills.” He needs neither intermediaries nor causes ( الوسائط والأسباب ). “When He decrees a thing, He merely says to it ‘Be!’ and it is.” He will teach him “the Book”—writing and the script—“and Wisdom”—prophecy or sound judgment—“and the Torah and the Gospel.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The signs granted to Jesus","html":"<p>“And [He will make him] a messenger to the Children of Israel.” Joseph was the first of the messengers among the Children of Israel, Jesus the last—upon them peace. A report attributed to the Prophet says: “I was sent after eight thousand prophets, four thousand of them from the Children of Israel.” When Jesus was sent to them, he said, “I have come to you with a sign from your Lord.” They asked what it was. He replied: “I fashion for you from clay something in the form of a bird; then I breathe into it and it becomes a bird, <strong>by God’s leave<\/strong>.” He used to form for them the shape of a bat, because it is the most complete of birds: it has breasts and teeth, menstruates and flies, making the miracle more striking. It flew while people looked at it and fell dead when out of their sight, to distinguish the act of the Real from that of the creature. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: These are transmitted exegetical descriptions; their claims about natural history have not been independently established.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N015\">[XIV-N015]<\/a><\/p><p>He then said: “And I heal the one born blind” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأكمه<\/bdi>)—and all the more one whose blindness came later—“and the leper” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأبرص<\/bdi>), one afflicted with whitish skin disease. These two were singled out because they were considered especially difficult to cure. Medicine was predominant in Jesus’ age, so he displayed a miracle of that kind. It is related that at times thousands of sick people gathered around him in one day: those able to come to him did so, and he visited those unable to move. He healed through supplication, on condition that they enter Islam [i.e., submit to God].<\/p><p>“And I bring the dead to life <strong>by God’s leave<\/strong>”—not by my own power, lest divinity be wrongly imputed to him, for the restoration of life lies beyond human capacity. It is related that he raised four people: Lazarus (<i>al-ʿĀzir<\/i>), his friend, after his sister sent word that he was dying. Jesus arrived after a journey of three days, only to find him dead. He went with the sister to the sealed rock-tomb, invoked God, and Lazarus stood up, his bodily fat dripping; he lived on and had children. The second was the son of an old woman whose funeral passed by Jesus. Through his prayer the young man sat up on the bier, descended from the men’s shoulders, dressed himself, carried the bier on his own shoulder and returned home, living long enough to have children. The third was the daughter of a collector of tithes: on being asked whether he could restore one who had died the day before, he prayed, and she lived and had offspring.<\/p><p>The fourth was Shem son of Noah. Jesus invoked God by His Greatest Name, and Shem came out of his grave, half his head white-haired. He asked, “Has the Hour come?” Jesus said, “No, but I invoked God and He gave you life. Why is your hair grey when it was not so in your time?” He answered that hearing the cry he supposed the Hour had come, and terror turned his hair grey. It is said Jesus raised the dead by the invocation “O Living, O Self-Subsisting” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يا حي يا قيوم<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N016\">[XIV-N016]<\/a><\/p><p>“And I tell you what you eat and what you store in your homes.” After witnessing his cures, some said that these were sorcery and demanded to know what they ate and saved. He would tell a man what he had eaten for his morning and evening meals. It is related that when he attended school, he told the boys what food their parents had prepared: “Go—your family’s meal today is such and such.” Asked how they knew, they said Jesus had informed them. Their parents confined them and warned them not to play with “this sorcerer.” When Jesus came looking for them, they denied the boys were there. He asked what the house contained. They said “Pigs.” Jesus said: “Then so shall they be.” When the door was opened, the boys were pigs. The people then intended to kill him, and his mother fled with him to Egypt. So relates al-Suddī. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N017\">[XIV-N017]<\/a><\/p><p>“Surely in that is a sign for you, if you are believers.” Those stubborn in unbelief gain nothing from miracles. “Confirming what came before me of the Torah”: I have come as its confirmer, attesting its truth, “and to make lawful for you some of what was forbidden to you” under Moses’ law—such as certain fats and fatty parts, camel flesh, and work on the Sabbath. This shows that Jesus superseded certain Torah rulings (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ناسخ للتوراة<\/bdi>), without compromising his confirmation of the Torah’s truth, just as one Qurʾānic ruling’s supersession of another does not compromise the truth of either. In reality abrogation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النسخ<\/bdi>) means clarifying that the operative period of a ruling has ended.<\/p><p>“I have come to you with a clear sign from your Lord,” which your own eyes have witnessed. Nothing remains but your obstinacy. “So be mindful of God and obey me.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“And [He will make him] a messenger to the Children of Israel.” Joseph was the first of the messengers among the Children of Israel, Jesus the last—upon them peace. A report attributed to the Prophet says: “I was sent after eight thousand prophets, four thousand of them from the Children of Israel.” When Jesus was sent to them, he said, “I have come to you with a sign from your Lord.” They asked what it was. He replied: “I fashion for you from clay something in the form of a bird; then I breathe into it and it becomes a bird, by God’s leave .” He used to form for them the shape of a bat, because it is the most complete of birds: it has breasts and teeth, menstruates and flies, making the miracle more striking. It flew while people looked at it and fell dead when out of their sight, to distinguish the act of the Real from that of the creature. [Translator’s note: These are transmitted exegetical descriptions; their claims about natural history have not been independently established.] [XIV-N015] He then said: “And I heal the one born blind” ( الأكمه )—and all the more one whose blindness came later—“and the leper” ( الأبرص ), one afflicted with whitish skin disease. These two were singled out because they were considered especially difficult to cure. Medicine was predominant in Jesus’ age, so he displayed a miracle of that kind. It is related that at times thousands of sick people gathered around him in one day: those able to come to him did so, and he visited those unable to move. He healed through supplication, on condition that they enter Islam [i.e., submit to God]. “And I bring the dead to life by God’s leave ”—not by my own power, lest divinity be wrongly imputed to him, for the restoration of life lies beyond human capacity. It is related that he raised four people: Lazarus ( al-ʿĀzir ), his friend, after his sister sent word that he was dying. Jesus arrived after a journey of three days, only to find him dead. He went with the sister to the sealed rock-tomb, invoked God, and Lazarus stood up, his bodily fat dripping; he lived on and had children. The second was the son of an old woman whose funeral passed by Jesus. Through his prayer the young man sat up on the bier, descended from the men’s shoulders, dressed himself, carried the bier on his own shoulder and returned home, living long enough to have children. The third was the daughter of a collector of tithes: on being asked whether he could restore one who had died the day before, he prayed, and she lived and had offspring. The fourth was Shem son of Noah. Jesus invoked God by His Greatest Name, and Shem came out of his grave, half his head white-haired. He asked, “Has the Hour come?” Jesus said, “No, but I invoked God and He gave you life. Why is your hair grey when it was not so in your time?” He answered that hearing the cry he supposed the Hour had come, and terror turned his hair grey. It is said Jesus raised the dead by the invocation “O Living, O Self-Subsisting” ( يا حي يا قيوم ). [XIV-N016] “And I tell you what you eat and what you store in your homes.” After witnessing his cures, some said that these were sorcery and demanded to know what they ate and saved. He would tell a man what he had eaten for his morning and evening meals. It is related that when he attended school, he told the boys what food their parents had prepared: “Go—your family’s meal today is such and such.” Asked how they knew, they said Jesus had informed them. Their parents confined them and warned them not to play with “this sorcerer.” When Jesus came looking for them, they denied the boys were there. He asked what the house contained. They said “Pigs.” Jesus said: “Then so shall they be.” When the door was opened, the boys were pigs. The people then intended to kill him, and his mother fled with him to Egypt. So relates al-Suddī. [XIV-N017] “Surely in that is a sign for you, if you are believers.” Those stubborn in unbelief gain nothing from miracles. “Confirming what came before me of the Torah”: I have come as its confirmer, attesting its truth, “and to make lawful for you some of what was forbidden to you” under Moses’ law—such as certain fats and fatty parts, camel flesh, and work on the Sabbath. This shows that Jesus superseded certain Torah rulings ( ناسخ للتوراة ), without compromising his confirmation of the Torah’s truth, just as one Qurʾānic ruling’s supersession of another does not compromise the truth of either. In reality abrogation ( النسخ ) means clarifying that the operative period of a ruling has ended. “I have come to you with a clear sign from your Lord,” which your own eyes have witnessed. Nothing remains but your obstinacy. “So be mindful of God and obey me.”"},{"type":"theology","title":"Divine unity and the straight way","html":"<p>He then calls them to divine unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد<\/bdi>) after establishing the evidence: “God is my Lord and your Lord; so worship Him,” and none besides Him. “This is a straight path,” without crookedness. Al-Bayḍāwī says: Having come with compelling miracles and manifest signs, “be mindful of God” by avoiding opposition “and obey me” in what I call you to. Jesus then begins his call, summed up in the words “God is my Lord and your Lord,” indicating the perfection of the theoretical power (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القوة النظرية<\/bdi>) through true belief, whose highest end is divine unity; and “so worship Him,” indicating the perfection of the practical power (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القوة العملية<\/bdi>) by persevering in obedience, performing commands and avoiding prohibitions. Uniting these two completes the path attested as straight. Its counterpart is the saying of the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him: “Say, ‘I believe in God,’ and then remain upright.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N018\">[XIV-N018]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then calls them to divine unity ( التوحيد ) after establishing the evidence: “God is my Lord and your Lord; so worship Him,” and none besides Him. “This is a straight path,” without crookedness. Al-Bayḍāwī says: Having come with compelling miracles and manifest signs, “be mindful of God” by avoiding opposition “and obey me” in what I call you to. Jesus then begins his call, summed up in the words “God is my Lord and your Lord,” indicating the perfection of the theoretical power ( القوة النظرية ) through true belief, whose highest end is divine unity; and “so worship Him,” indicating the perfection of the practical power ( القوة العملية ) by persevering in obedience, performing commands and avoiding prohibitions. Uniting these two completes the path attested as straight. Its counterpart is the saying of the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him: “Say, ‘I believe in God,’ and then remain upright.” [XIV-N018]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Mary’s consecration and the healing of hearts","html":"<p>Whoever turns wholly to his Master, withdrawing from his personal portions (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حظوظه<\/bdi>) and passions, spends his youth obeying his Lord and seeks throughout life the remedy of the heart, obtains the promised good in this world and the next, is purified of the stains of faults and vices, and is provided fruits of knowledge before whose comprehension the utmost reach of understanding dwindles. Mary the Virgin spent her youth obeying her Master. He drew her near, took charge of her, announced her election and purification, and commanded her to strive diligently out of gratitude. He then announced a second gift: an immaculate son, a distinguished master, the Spirit of God and the Word of God, with no father, ordinary cause, contrivance or toil. By God’s command he cured the blind and the leper and restored the dead by His leave. This was all through the blessing of complete devotion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الانقطاع<\/bdi>) and the secret of following faithfully (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاتباع<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>The Prophet is reported to have said: “Whoever devotes himself wholly to God, God suffices him for every burden and provides him from whence he does not expect; whoever gives himself over to the world, God leaves him to it.” One of the masters said: “The truth of spiritual struggle (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صدق المجاهدة<\/bdi>) is turning to Him, leaving everything besides Him.” Devotion to God in youth serves a person in old age, while youthful acts of disobedience draw calamity into old age.<\/p><p>Just as Jesus cured the blind and lepers by God’s leave, so the person wholly devoted to God cures sick hearts <strong>by God’s leave<\/strong>, gives life to dead hearts through God’s remembrance, and speaks of inwardly hidden matters and what the consciences of hearts conceal. He directs others to obedience and calls to God by state and speech, guides people to the straight path, and leads those who follow him into the Presence of bliss (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حضرة النعيم<\/bdi>). And success is through God; He guides to the straight way.<\/p><p>Once the announcement of Jesus was complete, he entered the world and reached the time of his mission. God sent him to the Children of Israel; they disbelieved in him. When their unbelief became manifest, he sought those who would help him towards God, as the Real indicates:<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever turns wholly to his Master, withdrawing from his personal portions ( حظوظه ) and passions, spends his youth obeying his Lord and seeks throughout life the remedy of the heart, obtains the promised good in this world and the next, is purified of the stains of faults and vices, and is provided fruits of knowledge before whose comprehension the utmost reach of understanding dwindles. Mary the Virgin spent her youth obeying her Master. He drew her near, took charge of her, announced her election and purification, and commanded her to strive diligently out of gratitude. He then announced a second gift: an immaculate son, a distinguished master, the Spirit of God and the Word of God, with no father, ordinary cause, contrivance or toil. By God’s command he cured the blind and the leper and restored the dead by His leave. This was all through the blessing of complete devotion ( الانقطاع ) and the secret of following faithfully ( الاتباع ). The Prophet is reported to have said: “Whoever devotes himself wholly to God, God suffices him for every burden and provides him from whence he does not expect; whoever gives himself over to the world, God leaves him to it.” One of the masters said: “The truth of spiritual struggle ( صدق المجاهدة ) is turning to Him, leaving everything besides Him.” Devotion to God in youth serves a person in old age, while youthful acts of disobedience draw calamity into old age. Just as Jesus cured the blind and lepers by God’s leave, so the person wholly devoted to God cures sick hearts by God’s leave , gives life to dead hearts through God’s remembrance, and speaks of inwardly hidden matters and what the consciences of hearts conceal. He directs others to obedience and calls to God by state and speech, guides people to the straight path, and leads those who follow him into the Presence of bliss ( حضرة النعيم ). And success is through God; He guides to the straight way. Once the announcement of Jesus was complete, he entered the world and reached the time of his mission. God sent him to the Children of Israel; they disbelieved in him. When their unbelief became manifest, he sought those who would help him towards God, as the Real indicates:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C355.1","page":355,"ar":"هو بياض يعتري الجلد.","en":"It is a whiteness that affects the skin.","anchor":"وضح"},{"id":"C355.2","page":355,"ar":"أي: بلوغ المريض المكان الذي فيه عيسى - عليه السلام -.","en":"That is, the sick person’s reaching the place where Jesus, peace be upon him, was.","anchor":"البلوغ"},{"id":"C355.3","page":355,"ar":"الودك: دسم اللحم ودهنه.","en":"Wadak: the fat or grease of meat.","anchor":"ودكه"},{"id":"C356.1","page":356,"ar":"الثروب: جمع ثرب، وهو شحم رقيق يغشى الكرش والأمعاء.","en":"Thurūb is the plural of tharb: a thin layer of fat covering the rumen and intestines.","anchor":"الثروب"}],"english_characters":11973,"arabic_characters":8676},{"id":"q3-52-54","q":"3:52–54","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:52–54 · The disciples, divine stratagem and the Path’s companions","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 357–359","cairo":"I:357–359","source_page_first":357,"source_page_last":359,"ar":"فَلَمَّا أَحَسَّ عِيسى مِنْهُمُ الْكُفْرَ قالَ مَنْ أَنْصارِي إِلَى اللَّهِ قالَ الْحَوارِيُّونَ نَحْنُ أَنْصارُ اللَّهِ آمَنَّا بِاللَّهِ وَاشْهَدْ بِأَنَّا مُسْلِمُونَ (52) رَبَّنا آمَنَّا بِما أَنْزَلْتَ وَاتَّبَعْنَا الرَّسُولَ فَاكْتُبْنا مَعَ الشَّاهِدِينَ (53) وَمَكَرُوا وَمَكَرَ اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُ خَيْرُ الْماكِرِينَ (54)\nقلت: (من أنصاري إلى الله) : الجار يتعلق بحال محذوفة، أي: ذاهباً إلى الله إلى نَصْر دينه، أو مُضيفاً نفسه إلى الله، أو ملتجئاً إلى الله، أو يتعلق ب- (أنصاري) مضمِّناً معنى الإضافة، أي: من يضيف نفسه إلى الله في نصره. وحوارى الرجل: خاصته، الذين يستعين بهم في نوائبه، وفى الحديث عنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «لكلِّ نبي حَوَاري، وحَوارِيي: الزّبير» . وحواريو عيسى: أصحابه الذين نصروه، وسموا بذلك لخلوص نيتهم ونقاء سريرتهم. والحَوَرُ: البياض الخالص، وكل شيء بيَّضته فقد حوَّرْته، ويقال للبيضاء من النساء: حوارية. وقيل: كان الحواريون قًصَّارين(1) ، يُحَوِّرُون الثياب، أي: يبيضونها، وقيل: كانوا ملوكاً يلبسون البياض.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فَلَمَّا أَحَسَّ عِيسى من بني إسرائيل الْكُفْرَ، وتحققه تَحَقُّقَ ما يدرك بالحواس، بعد ما بُعث إليهم، وأرادوا قتله، فرَّ منهم واستنصر عليهم، وقالَ مَنْ أَنْصارِي ملجئاً إِلَى اللَّهِ، أو ذاهباً إلى نصر دينه، قالَ الْحَوارِيُّونَ نَحْنُ أَنْصارُ اللَّهِ أي: أنصار دينه، آمَنَّا بِاللَّهِ وَاشْهَدْ علينا بأننا مُسْلِمُونَ لتشهد لنا يوم القيامة، حين يشهد الرسل لقومهم، رَبَّنا آمَنَّا بِما أَنْزَلْتَ على نبيك من الأحكام، وَاتَّبَعْنَا الرَّسُولَ عيسى عليه السلام، فَاكْتُبْنا مَعَ الشَّاهِدِينَ بوحدانيتك، أو مع الذين يشهدون لأنبيائك\nبالصدق، أو مع الأنبياء الذين يشهدون لأتباعهم، أو مع أمة محمد- عليه الصلاة والسلام- فإنهم شهداء على الناس.\nقال عطاء: سَلَمَتْ مريمُ عيسى إلى أعمال شتى، وآخر ما دفعته إلى الحواريين، وكانوا قصَّارين وصباغين، فأراد مُعلّم عيسى السفر، فقال لعيسى: عندي ثياب كثيرة مختلفة الألوان، وقد علمتك الحرفة فاصبغها، فطبخ جُبّاً واحداً، وأدخل فيه جميع الثياب، وقال لها: كوني على ما أريد، فقدم الحواريُ، والثياب كلها في الجب، فلما رآها قال: قد أفسدتها، فأخرج عيسى ثوباً أصفر، وأحمر، وأخضر، إلى غير ذلك، فعجب الحواري، وعلم أنَّ ذلك من الله تعالى، ودعا الناس إليه، وآمنوا به، ونصروه، فهم الحواريون.\nولما أخرجه بنو إسرائيل عاد إليهم مع الحواريين، وصاح فيهم بالدعوة، فهمُّوا بقتله، وتواطئوا عليه، وَمَكَرُوا أي: دبروا الحيل في قتله، وَمَكَرَ اللَّهُ بهم، أي: استدرجهم حتى قتلوا صاحبهم، ورُفع عيسى عليه السلام، فالمكر في الأصل: هو حيلة يجلب بها غيره إلى مضرة. ولا تُسند إلى الله إلا على حسب المقابلة والازدواج، كقوله:\nيُخادِعُونَ اللَّهَ وَهُوَ خادِعُهُمْ\n، وقوله: اللَّهُ يَسْتَهْزِئُ بِهِمْ، وَاللَّهُ خَيْرُ الْماكِرِينَ. أي: أشدهم مكراً، وأقواهم على إيصال الضرر من حيث لا يحتسب، أو أفضل المجازين بالعقوبة لأنه لا أحد أقدر على ذلك منه.\nتنبيه: قيل للجنيد رضى الله عنه: كيف رَضِيَ المكرَ لنفسه، وقد عابه على غيره؟ قال: لا أدري، ولكن أنشدني فلان للطبرانية:\nفديتُك قد جُبِلْتُ على هواكَ ... ونفْسِي ما تَحِنُّ إلى سِوَاكَ\nأُحِبّك، لا بِبَعْضِي بل بكلّى ... وإن لم يُبْقِ حُبُّكَ لي حِرَاكَا\nوَيَقْبُحُ مِنْ سِوَاكَ الْفِعْلُ عِنْدي ... وتَفْعَلُهُ فَيَحْسُنُ مِنْكَ ذَاكَ(1)\nفقال له السائل: أسألُك عن القرآن، وتجيبني بشعر الطبرانية؟ قال: ويحك، قد أجبتك إن كنت تعقل. إنَّ تخليته إياهم مع المكرية، مكرٌ منه بهم. هـ.\nقلت: وجه الشاهد في قوله: (وتفعله فيحسن منك ذاك) ، ومضمن جوابه: أن فعل الله كله حسن في غاية الإتقان، لا عيب فيه ولا نقصان، كما قال صاحب العينية:\nوَكلُّ قبِيح إنْ نَسَبْتَ لِحُسْنِهِ ... أَتَتْكَ مَعَانِي الْحُسْنِ فِيهِ تُسَارعُ\nيُكَمِّلُ نُقصَانَ الْقَبِيحِ جَمَالُهُ ... فَما ثَمَّ نُقْصَانٌ وَلاَ ثَمَّ بَاشِعُ\nوتخليته تعالى إياهم مع المكر، تسبب عنه الرفع إلى السماء، وإبقاء عيسى حيّاً إلى آخر الزمان، حتى ينزل خليفة عن نبينا- عليه الصلاة والسلام-، فكان ذلك في غاية الكمال والإتقان، لكن لا يفطن لهذا إلا أهل العرفان.\nالإشارة: يجب على المريد الصادق الذي يطلب دواء قلبه، أن يفر من الوطن الذي يظهر فيه الإنكار، إلى الوطن الذي يكثر فيه الإقرار، يفر إلى من يعينه على نصر الدين من الأبرار المقربين، الذين جعلهم الله حواري الدين، ففي الحديث الصحيح: «خَيْرُ مالِ المُسْلِم غَنَمٌ يَتْبَعُ بها شَعَفَ(1) الجِبَالِ يَفِرُّ مِنَ الْفِتَنِ» . فالمؤمن يفر بدينه من شاهق جبل إلى شاهق جبل حتى يدركه الموت، وما زالت الأكابر تفر بنفسها إلى شواهق الجبال، يهربون من حس الدنيا وشغبها، ولا يرافقون إلا من يستعين بهم على ذكر الله، وهم أهل التجريد، الذين اصطفاهم الله لخالص التوحيد، فروا إلى الله فآواهم الله، قالوا: (آمنا بالله وأشهد بأنا مسلمون) منقادون لما تريد منا، (ربنا آمنا بما أنزلت) من الأحكام الجلالية والجمالية قد عرفناك في جميع الحالات، (فاكتبنا مع الشاهدين) لحضرتك، المنعمين بشهود ذاتك، ومن مكر بنا من القواطع الخفية فَغَيَّبنا عنه بشهود أنوارك القدسية، وانصرنا فإنك خير الناصرين، ولا تدعنا مع مكر الماكرين يا رب العالمين.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى رفع عيسى إلى السماء فقال:","quran_ar":"فَلَمَّا أَحَسَّ عِيسى مِنْهُمُ الْكُفْرَ قالَ مَنْ أَنْصارِي إِلَى اللَّهِ قالَ الْحَوارِيُّونَ نَحْنُ أَنْصارُ اللَّهِ آمَنَّا بِاللَّهِ وَاشْهَدْ بِأَنَّا مُسْلِمُونَ (52) رَبَّنا آمَنَّا بِما أَنْزَلْتَ وَاتَّبَعْنَا الرَّسُولَ فَاكْتُبْنا مَعَ الشَّاهِدِينَ (53) وَمَكَرُوا وَمَكَرَ اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُ خَيْرُ الْماكِرِينَ (54)","quran_en":"52. When Jesus sensed unbelief in them, he said, “Who are my helpers towards God?” The disciples said, “We are God’s helpers. We believe in God; bear witness that we submit.” 53. “Our Lord, we believe in what You have sent down and we follow the Messenger; so write us among the witnesses.” 54. They plotted, and God planned; and God is the best of planners.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"“My helpers towards God” and “disciples”","html":"<p>I say: in “Who are my helpers towards God?” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">من أنصاري إلى الله<\/bdi>), the preposition attaches to an omitted circumstantial expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>): “as one going to God,” that is, to the aid of His religion; or “as one attaching himself to God”; or “as one seeking refuge in God.” Alternatively it attaches to “my helpers,” understood in the sense of joining or attaching oneself: “Who will join himself to God in helping His religion?”<\/p><p>A man’s <i>ḥawārī<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حواري<\/bdi>) is one of his intimates upon whose assistance he relies in adversity. The Prophet said: “Every prophet has a <i>ḥawārī<\/i>; my <i>ḥawārī<\/i> is al-Zubayr.” Jesus’ <i>ḥawāriyyūn<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحواريون<\/bdi>) were the companions who supported him. They were so called because of their pure intentions and unblemished innermost dispositions. The root <i>ḥawar<\/i> denotes pure whiteness: anything you whiten, you have <i>ḥawwarta<\/i>; a fair-complexioned woman can be called <i>ḥawāriyya<\/i>. Some say they were launderers or fullers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قصّارون<\/bdi>) who whitened cloth; others say they were kings dressed in white.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: in “Who are my helpers towards God?” ( من أنصاري إلى الله ), the preposition attaches to an omitted circumstantial expression ( حال ): “as one going to God,” that is, to the aid of His religion; or “as one attaching himself to God”; or “as one seeking refuge in God.” Alternatively it attaches to “my helpers,” understood in the sense of joining or attaching oneself: “Who will join himself to God in helping His religion?” A man’s ḥawārī ( حواري ) is one of his intimates upon whose assistance he relies in adversity. The Prophet said: “Every prophet has a ḥawārī ; my ḥawārī is al-Zubayr.” Jesus’ ḥawāriyyūn ( الحواريون ) were the companions who supported him. They were so called because of their pure intentions and unblemished innermost dispositions. The root ḥawar denotes pure whiteness: anything you whiten, you have ḥawwarta ; a fair-complexioned woman can be called ḥawāriyya . Some say they were launderers or fullers ( قصّارون ) who whitened cloth; others say they were kings dressed in white."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The disciples’ confession and the attempt upon Jesus","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “When Jesus sensed unbelief” among the Children of Israel—apprehending it with the certainty of something perceived by the senses—after his mission had begun and they intended to kill him, he fled and sought assistance against them. “He said: Who will help me, turning towards God?” Or: who will go to the aid of His religion? “The disciples replied: We are helpers of God,” meaning helpers of His religion. “We believe in God, and bear witness that we submit” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مسلمون<\/bdi>), so that you may testify on our behalf on the Day of Resurrection, when messengers testify concerning their communities.<\/p><p>“Our Lord, we believe in what You have sent down” of rulings upon Your prophet “and follow the Messenger,” Jesus—peace be upon him. “Write us among the witnesses”: witnesses to Your oneness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وحدانيتك<\/bdi>); or among those who testify to the truthfulness of Your prophets; or with the prophets who bear witness for their followers; or among Muḥammad’s community, who are witnesses over people.<\/p><p>ʿAṭāʾ relates that Mary handed Jesus over to learn various crafts, the last apprenticeship being with the disciples, who worked as cloth-finishers and dyers. When Jesus’ teacher was about to travel, he left him many garments of different colours to dye. Jesus heated a single vat, placed all the garments in it and bade them take the colours he wished. On the teacher’s return, the man feared that all had been ruined. Jesus took out yellow, red, green and other garments, each according to its intended colour. Amazed, the teacher knew that the act was from God, called people to him, and they believed and gave him support; hence they became disciples. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N019\">[XIV-N019]<\/a><\/p><p>After the Children of Israel expelled him, Jesus returned with the disciples and proclaimed his mission to them. They sought to kill him and conspired: “They plotted” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مكروا<\/bdi>), contriving means to put him to death; “and God planned against them,” allowing them to kill one of their own party while Jesus was raised. In its original usage <i>makr<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المكر<\/bdi>) is a device by which one draws another into harm. It is predicated of God only in response and correspondence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">على سبيل المقابلة والازدواج<\/bdi>), as in “They seek to deceive God, but He is their deceiver” [4:142] and “God mocks them” [2:15]. “God is the best of planners” means either the mightiest in strategic action, most able to bring about punishment from unsuspected quarters, or the best in recompensing wrongdoing, since none is more able than He.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “When Jesus sensed unbelief” among the Children of Israel—apprehending it with the certainty of something perceived by the senses—after his mission had begun and they intended to kill him, he fled and sought assistance against them. “He said: Who will help me, turning towards God?” Or: who will go to the aid of His religion? “The disciples replied: We are helpers of God,” meaning helpers of His religion. “We believe in God, and bear witness that we submit” ( مسلمون ), so that you may testify on our behalf on the Day of Resurrection, when messengers testify concerning their communities. “Our Lord, we believe in what You have sent down” of rulings upon Your prophet “and follow the Messenger,” Jesus—peace be upon him. “Write us among the witnesses”: witnesses to Your oneness ( وحدانيتك ); or among those who testify to the truthfulness of Your prophets; or with the prophets who bear witness for their followers; or among Muḥammad’s community, who are witnesses over people. ʿAṭāʾ relates that Mary handed Jesus over to learn various crafts, the last apprenticeship being with the disciples, who worked as cloth-finishers and dyers. When Jesus’ teacher was about to travel, he left him many garments of different colours to dye. Jesus heated a single vat, placed all the garments in it and bade them take the colours he wished. On the teacher’s return, the man feared that all had been ruined. Jesus took out yellow, red, green and other garments, each according to its intended colour. Amazed, the teacher knew that the act was from God, called people to him, and they believed and gave him support; hence they became disciples. [XIV-N019] After the Children of Israel expelled him, Jesus returned with the disciples and proclaimed his mission to them. They sought to kill him and conspired: “They plotted” ( مكروا ), contriving means to put him to death; “and God planned against them,” allowing them to kill one of their own party while Jesus was raised. In its original usage makr ( المكر ) is a device by which one draws another into harm. It is predicated of God only in response and correspondence ( على سبيل المقابلة والازدواج ), as in “They seek to deceive God, but He is their deceiver” [4:142] and “God mocks them” [2:15]. “God is the best of planners” means either the mightiest in strategic action, most able to bring about punishment from unsuspected quarters, or the best in recompensing wrongdoing, since none is more able than He."},{"type":"theology","title":"Why “planning” is predicated of God","html":"<p><strong>Notice (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تنبيه<\/bdi>).<\/strong> Al-Junayd was asked: “How can He accept <i>makr<\/i> for Himself when He reproves it in others?” He answered, “I do not know—but so-and-so recited for me verses of al-Ṭabarāniyya”:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">For you I would give myself: I was fashioned in desire for you,<br/>and my soul yearns for none besides you.<br/>I love you not with one part, but with my whole being,<br/>though love for you should leave no movement in me.<br/>In another I find an act reprehensible;<br/>you do that very act, and from you it seems fair.<\/blockquote><p>The questioner protested: “I ask you about the Qurʾān and you answer with the poetry of al-Ṭabarāniyya?” Al-Junayd replied: “Alas for you! I have answered you, if you can understand. God’s letting them persist in their plotting was itself His <i>makr<\/i> against them.” End of quotation.<\/p><p>I say: the relevant witness is “you do that very act, and from you it seems fair.” The answer implies that every act of God is good and perfectly accomplished, without deficiency or defect. The poet of the <i>ʿAyniyya<\/i> says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Whenever you trace the ugly thing to His beauty,<br/>meanings of beauty hasten forth within it.<br/>His beauty perfects what seemed defective;<br/>no deficiency is left, nor any ugliness.<\/blockquote><p>His leaving them to their intrigue resulted in Jesus’ being raised to heaven and preserved alive until the last age, when he descends as the deputy of our Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him. This was an act of the highest perfection and wisdom, although only the people of gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل العرفان<\/bdi>) discern it. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N020\">[XIV-N020]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Notice ( تنبيه ). Al-Junayd was asked: “How can He accept makr for Himself when He reproves it in others?” He answered, “I do not know—but so-and-so recited for me verses of al-Ṭabarāniyya”: For you I would give myself: I was fashioned in desire for you, and my soul yearns for none besides you. I love you not with one part, but with my whole being, though love for you should leave no movement in me. In another I find an act reprehensible; you do that very act, and from you it seems fair. The questioner protested: “I ask you about the Qurʾān and you answer with the poetry of al-Ṭabarāniyya?” Al-Junayd replied: “Alas for you! I have answered you, if you can understand. God’s letting them persist in their plotting was itself His makr against them.” End of quotation. I say: the relevant witness is “you do that very act, and from you it seems fair.” The answer implies that every act of God is good and perfectly accomplished, without deficiency or defect. The poet of the ʿAyniyya says: Whenever you trace the ugly thing to His beauty, meanings of beauty hasten forth within it. His beauty perfects what seemed defective; no deficiency is left, nor any ugliness. His leaving them to their intrigue resulted in Jesus’ being raised to heaven and preserved alive until the last age, when he descends as the deputy of our Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him. This was an act of the highest perfection and wisdom, although only the people of gnosis ( أهل العرفان ) discern it. [XIV-N020]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Fleeing denial and finding helpers on the Way","html":"<p>The sincere aspirant who seeks a remedy for the heart ought to flee a land where denial prevails to one where affirmation is widespread; flee towards righteous ones brought near to God who help him defend the religion—the disciples of religion whom God has appointed. The sound hadith says: “The best wealth of a Muslim is sheep with which he follows the mountain peaks, fleeing tribulations.” The believer flees with his religion from one mountain summit to another until death overtakes him. The eminent masters, too, have withdrawn to the mountains, away from the sensible clamor of the world. They accompany only those who help them remember God: the people of detachment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل التجريد<\/bdi>), chosen for pure divine unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خالص التوحيد<\/bdi>). They fled towards God, and He sheltered them.<\/p><p>“We believe in God. Bear witness that we submit”—we yield to what You desire of us. “Our Lord, we believe in what You have sent down” in decrees of majesty and beauty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأحكام الجلالية والجمالية<\/bdi>); we have known You in every condition. “Write us among the witnesses”—those who witness Your Presence, graced with witnessing Your Essence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شهود ذاتك<\/bdi>). Whatever hidden impediments (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القواطع الخفية<\/bdi>) plot against us, make us absent from them through witnessing Your holy lights. Help us, for You are the best of helpers; do not abandon us to the schemes of schemers, Lord of the worlds.<\/p><p>The Real then mentions Jesus’ elevation to heaven:<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The sincere aspirant who seeks a remedy for the heart ought to flee a land where denial prevails to one where affirmation is widespread; flee towards righteous ones brought near to God who help him defend the religion—the disciples of religion whom God has appointed. The sound hadith says: “The best wealth of a Muslim is sheep with which he follows the mountain peaks, fleeing tribulations.” The believer flees with his religion from one mountain summit to another until death overtakes him. The eminent masters, too, have withdrawn to the mountains, away from the sensible clamor of the world. They accompany only those who help them remember God: the people of detachment ( أهل التجريد ), chosen for pure divine unity ( خالص التوحيد ). They fled towards God, and He sheltered them. “We believe in God. Bear witness that we submit”—we yield to what You desire of us. “Our Lord, we believe in what You have sent down” in decrees of majesty and beauty ( الأحكام الجلالية والجمالية ); we have known You in every condition. “Write us among the witnesses”—those who witness Your Presence, graced with witnessing Your Essence ( شهود ذاتك ). Whatever hidden impediments ( القواطع الخفية ) plot against us, make us absent from them through witnessing Your holy lights. Help us, for You are the best of helpers; do not abandon us to the schemes of schemers, Lord of the worlds. The Real then mentions Jesus’ elevation to heaven:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C357.1","page":357,"ar":"القصار: المبيض للثياب، وهو الذي يهيئ النسيج بعد نسجه، ببله ودقه بالقصرة - التي هي القطعة من الخشب.","en":"A qaṣṣār is a bleacher of cloth: one who finishes fabric after it is woven by wetting it and beating it with a qaṣra, a piece of wood.","anchor":"قًصَّارين"},{"id":"C358.1","page":358,"ar":"القصة ذكرها مختصرة أبو حيان في التفسير 2/496 مقتصرا على البيت الثالث.","en":"Abū Ḥayyān mentions the story in abridged form in his commentary,2:496, quoting only the third verse.","anchor":"وتَفْعَلُهُ فَيَحْسُنُ مِنْكَ ذَاكَ"},{"id":"C359.1","page":359,"ar":"(شعف)، بفتح الشين والعين: جمع شعفة، وهي من كل شيء: أعلاه. والمراد بها هنا: رؤوس الجبال.","en":"Shaʿaf, with fatḥa on shīn and ʿayn, is the plural of shaʿafa, the uppermost part of anything. Here it means the mountain peaks.","anchor":"شَعَفَ"}],"english_characters":6424,"arabic_characters":4541},{"id":"q3-55-58","q":"3:55–58","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:55–58 · Jesus’ elevation and the purification of the spirit","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 359–361","cairo":"I:359–361","source_page_first":359,"source_page_last":361,"ar":"إِذْ قالَ اللَّهُ يا عِيسى إِنِّي مُتَوَفِّيكَ وَرافِعُكَ إِلَيَّ وَمُطَهِّرُكَ مِنَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وَجاعِلُ الَّذِينَ اتَّبَعُوكَ فَوْقَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا إِلى يَوْمِ الْقِيامَةِ ثُمَّ إِلَيَّ مَرْجِعُكُمْ فَأَحْكُمُ بَيْنَكُمْ فِيما كُنْتُمْ فِيهِ تَخْتَلِفُونَ (55) فَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا فَأُعَذِّبُهُمْ عَذاباً شَدِيداً فِي الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ وَما لَهُمْ مِنْ ناصِرِينَ (56) وَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحاتِ فَيُوَفِّيهِمْ أُجُورَهُمْ وَاللَّهُ لا يُحِبُّ الظَّالِمِينَ (57) ذلِكَ نَتْلُوهُ عَلَيْكَ مِنَ الْآياتِ وَالذِّكْرِ الْحَكِيمِ (58)\nقلت: (إذ قال) : ظرف لمقدر، أي: اذكر، أو وقع ذلك إذ قال، أو لمكروا، (متوفيك) أي: رافعك إليَّ وافياً تاماً، من قولهم: توفيت كذا واستوفيته: قبضته وافياً تامّاً، أو مميتك عن الشهوات العائقة عن العروج إلى عالم الملكوت، أو منيمك بدليل قوله تعالى: وَهُوَ الَّذِي يَتَوَفَّاكُمْ بِاللَّيْلِ، رُوِيَ أنه رُفع نائماً، والإجماع على أنه لم يمت،\nقال تعالى: وَما قَتَلُوهُ وَما صَلَبُوهُ وَلكِنْ شُبِّهَ لَهُمْ، وقوله: (ذلك) مبتدأ، و (نتلوه) : خبر، و (من الآيات) :\nحال، أو (من الآيات) : خبر، و (نتلوه) : حال، أو خبر بعد خبر.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: اذكر إِذْ قالَ اللَّهُ لعيسى عليه السلام لما أراد رفعه: يا عِيسى إِنِّي مُتَوَفِّيكَ، أي:\nقابضك إليّ ببدنك تامّاً، وَرافِعُكَ إِلَيَّ أي: إلى محل كرامتي ومقر ملائكتي، وَمُطَهِّرُكَ مِنَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا أي: من مخالطة دنس كفرهم، وَجاعِلُ الَّذِينَ اتَّبَعُوكَ ممن صدق بنبوتك من النصارى والمسلمين، وقال قتادة والشعبي والربيع: هم أهل الإسلام. هـ. فو الله ما اتبعه من ادعاه ربا، فمن تبع دينه حقا جعل فَوْقَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا به من اليهود إِلى يَوْمِ الْقِيامَةِ يغلبونهم بالحجة والسيف. وقد حقق الله فيهم هذا الأمر، فإن اليهود لم ترفع لهم راية قط، ولم يتفق لهم ملك ولا دولة إلى زمننا هذا(1) .\nثم قال تعالى: ثُمَّ إِلَيَّ مَرْجِعُكُمْ بالبعث، فَأَحْكُمُ بَيْنَكُمْ فِيما كُنْتُمْ فِيهِ تَخْتَلِفُونَ من أمر الدين وأمر عيسى. فَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا فَأُعَذِّبُهُمْ عَذاباً شَدِيداً فِي الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ أي: فأجمع لهم عذاب الآخرة لعذاب الدنيا الذي أصابهم فيها من القتل والسبي. وَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحاتِ فَيُوَفِّيهِمْ أُجُورَهُمْ في الدارين بالنصر والعز في الدنيا، وبالرضا والرضوان في الآخرة، وَاللَّهُ لا يُحِبُّ الظَّالِمِينَ لا يرضى فعلهم ولا يقربهم إليه.\nذلِكَ الذي ذكرت لك من نبأ عيسى ومريم ومن ذكر قبلهما، نَتْلُوهُ عَلَيْكَ مِنَ الْآياتِ أي: العلامات الدالة على صدقك، لأنها أخبار عن أمور لم تشاهدها ولم تقرأها في كتاب، بل هي من الذِّكْرِ الْحَكِيمِ، وهو القرآن المبين.\nالإشارة: كل مَن طَهَّر سره من الأكدار، وقدس روحه من دنس الأغيار، ورفع همته عن هذه الدار، عرج الله بروحه إلى سماء الملكوت، ورفع سره إلى مشاهدة سنا الجبروت، وبقي ذكره حيّاً لا يموت، وجعل من انتسب إليه في عين الرعاية والتعظيم، وفي محل الرفعة والتكريم، قال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «هَاجِرُوا تكسبوا العز لأولادكم» ، فمن هاجر وطن الحظوظ والشهوات، والركون إلى العوائد والمألوفات، عرجت روحه إلى سماء القدس ومحل الأنس، وتمكن من العز الذي لا يفنى، ينسحب عليه وعلى أولاده ومن انتسب إليه إلى أن يرث الله الأرض ومَن عليها، (وهو خير الوارثين) . هذه سُنة الله في خلقه، لأنهم نصروا دين الله ورفعوا كلمة الله، فنصرهم الله، ورفعهم الله، قال تعالى: إِنْ تَنْصُرُوا اللَّهَ يَنْصُرْكُمْ، وقال تعالى: وَجَعَلَ كَلِمَةَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا السُّفْلى وَكَلِمَةُ اللَّهِ هِيَ الْعُلْيا. وفي الحكم: «إن أردت أن يكون لك عز لا يفنى، فلا تستعزن بعز يفنى» . والله تعالى أعلم.\nوقال القشيري: الإشارة فيه: إني متوفيك عنك وقابضك منك، ورافعك عن نعوت البشرية، ومطهرك عن إرادتك بالكلية، حتى تكون مصدقاً لنا بنا، ولا يكون لك من اختيارك شيء، وتكون إسبال التولي عليك قائماً، وبهذا الوصف كان يظهر على يده إحياء الموتى، وما كانت تلك الأحداث حاصلة إلا بالقدرة عليه. هـ. وقال الورتجبي:\nمتوفيك عن رسم الحدوثية، ورافعك إليَّ بنعت الربوبية، ومطهرك عن شوائب البشرية. هـ.\nثم ذكر نشأة عيسى وخلقه، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِذْ قالَ اللَّهُ يا عِيسى إِنِّي مُتَوَفِّيكَ وَرافِعُكَ إِلَيَّ وَمُطَهِّرُكَ مِنَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وَجاعِلُ الَّذِينَ اتَّبَعُوكَ فَوْقَ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا إِلى يَوْمِ الْقِيامَةِ ثُمَّ إِلَيَّ مَرْجِعُكُمْ فَأَحْكُمُ بَيْنَكُمْ فِيما كُنْتُمْ فِيهِ تَخْتَلِفُونَ (55) فَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا فَأُعَذِّبُهُمْ عَذاباً شَدِيداً فِي الدُّنْيا وَالْآخِرَةِ وَما لَهُمْ مِنْ ناصِرِينَ (56) وَأَمَّا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحاتِ فَيُوَفِّيهِمْ أُجُورَهُمْ وَاللَّهُ لا يُحِبُّ الظَّالِمِينَ (57) ذلِكَ نَتْلُوهُ عَلَيْكَ مِنَ الْآياتِ وَالذِّكْرِ الْحَكِيمِ (58)","quran_en":"55. When God said, “Jesus, I shall take you fully and raise you to Me and purify you of those who disbelieve, and I shall set those who follow you above those who disbelieve until the Day of Resurrection. Then to Me you shall return, and I shall judge between you in that about which you differed. 56. As for those who disbelieve, I shall punish them severely in this world and the Hereafter, and they shall have no helpers. 57. But those who believe and perform righteous deeds, He will give them their rewards in full. God loves not the wrongdoers.” 58. This is what We recite to you from the signs and the wise Remembrance.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The meanings of tawaffī and the demonstrative","html":"<p>I say: “When He said” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إذ قال<\/bdi>) is governed by something understood: “remember”; or, “that took place when He said”; or it depends on “they plotted.” “I shall take you fully” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مُتَوَفِّيكَ<\/bdi>) may mean “I shall raise you to Me complete and intact”: one says <i>tawaffaytu<\/i> or <i>istawfaytu<\/i> something when one receives it in full. Or it means “I shall cause you to die to the desires that impede ascent to the world of malakūt” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الملكوت<\/bdi>). Or it means “I shall cause you to sleep,” as indicated by “He it is who takes you [in sleep] by night” [6:60]. It is related that he was raised while sleeping. Ibn ʿAjība states a consensus that Jesus did not die, citing “They did not kill him or crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them” [4:157].  <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N021\">[XIV-N021]<\/a><\/p><p>“This” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذلك<\/bdi>) is the subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المبتدأ<\/bdi>) and “We recite it” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نتلوه<\/bdi>) the predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخبر<\/bdi>); “from the signs” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">من الآيات<\/bdi>) may be an accusative of state (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>). Alternatively “from the signs” is the predicate and “We recite it” a circumstantial clause; or there are two predicates.<\/p><p class=\"translator-note-ref\">[Translator’s print-source notes: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/R07-14\">[R07-14]<\/a>]<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “When He said” ( إذ قال ) is governed by something understood: “remember”; or, “that took place when He said”; or it depends on “they plotted.” “I shall take you fully” ( مُتَوَفِّيكَ ) may mean “I shall raise you to Me complete and intact”: one says tawaffaytu or istawfaytu something when one receives it in full. Or it means “I shall cause you to die to the desires that impede ascent to the world of malakūt” ( عالم الملكوت ). Or it means “I shall cause you to sleep,” as indicated by “He it is who takes you [in sleep] by night” [6:60]. It is related that he was raised while sleeping. Ibn ʿAjība states a consensus that Jesus did not die, citing “They did not kill him or crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them” [4:157]. [XIV-N021] “This” ( ذلك ) is the subject ( المبتدأ ) and “We recite it” ( نتلوه ) the predicate ( الخبر ); “from the signs” ( من الآيات ) may be an accusative of state ( حال ). Alternatively “from the signs” is the predicate and “We recite it” a circumstantial clause; or there are two predicates. [Translator’s print-source notes: [R07-14] ]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The raising, judgment and recompense","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Remember when God said” to Jesus, as He intended to raise him: “Jesus, I shall take you fully”—receive you to Myself, body and all, without diminution—“and raise you to Me,” to the place of My honour and the abode of My angels, “and purify you of those who disbelieve,” freeing you from companionship polluted by their unbelief. “I shall set those who follow you,” who believe in your prophethood, Christians and Muslims; though Qatāda, al-Shaʿbī and al-Rabīʿ say it means the Muslims. End of report. By God, whoever claims Jesus as Lord has not truly followed him. Those who really follow his religion are placed “above those who disbelieve” in him, the Jews, “until the Day of Resurrection,” overcoming them in argument and by the sword. He adds: This matter has been realised concerning them, for the Jews have not raised a standard, nor possessed kingship or a state, down to our own time. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N022\">[XIV-N022]<\/a><\/p><p>“Then to Me is your return” through resurrection, “and I shall judge between you concerning that in which you differed” over religion and Jesus. “Those who disbelieve, I shall punish with a severe punishment in this world and the Hereafter”—joining the punishment of the next world to the killing and captivity they endured here. “Those who believe and perform righteous deeds, He will give their rewards in full” in the two abodes: help and honour here, satisfaction and good pleasure in the next. “God loves not the wrongdoers”: He does not approve their acts or draw them near.<\/p><p>“This” narrative that I have told you about Jesus, Mary and those before them “We recite to you from the signs,” indications of your truthfulness because they recount events you did not witness or read in books; indeed it is of “the Wise Remembrance” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الذِّكْر الحكيم<\/bdi>), the clear Qurʾān.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Remember when God said” to Jesus, as He intended to raise him: “Jesus, I shall take you fully”—receive you to Myself, body and all, without diminution—“and raise you to Me,” to the place of My honour and the abode of My angels, “and purify you of those who disbelieve,” freeing you from companionship polluted by their unbelief. “I shall set those who follow you,” who believe in your prophethood, Christians and Muslims; though Qatāda, al-Shaʿbī and al-Rabīʿ say it means the Muslims. End of report. By God, whoever claims Jesus as Lord has not truly followed him. Those who really follow his religion are placed “above those who disbelieve” in him, the Jews, “until the Day of Resurrection,” overcoming them in argument and by the sword. He adds: This matter has been realised concerning them, for the Jews have not raised a standard, nor possessed kingship or a state, down to our own time. [XIV-N022] “Then to Me is your return” through resurrection, “and I shall judge between you concerning that in which you differed” over religion and Jesus. “Those who disbelieve, I shall punish with a severe punishment in this world and the Hereafter”—joining the punishment of the next world to the killing and captivity they endured here. “Those who believe and perform righteous deeds, He will give their rewards in full” in the two abodes: help and honour here, satisfaction and good pleasure in the next. “God loves not the wrongdoers”: He does not approve their acts or draw them near. “This” narrative that I have told you about Jesus, Mary and those before them “We recite to you from the signs,” indications of your truthfulness because they recount events you did not witness or read in books; indeed it is of “the Wise Remembrance” ( الذِّكْر الحكيم ), the clear Qurʾān."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Ascending beyond desires and enduring through transmission","html":"<p>Whoever purifies the innermost secret (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السرّ<\/bdi>) of turbidity, sanctifies the spirit from the impurity of otherness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأغيار<\/bdi>), and lifts his aspiration beyond this abode, God causes his spirit to ascend to the heaven of malakūt and elevates his secret to the witnessing of the splendour of jabarūt (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مشاهدة سنا الجبروت<\/bdi>). His renown remains alive after death, and those attached to him enjoy care, reverence, dignity and honour. The Prophet is reported to have said: “Emigrate, and you will gain honour for your children.” Whoever leaves the homeland of personal portions and passions (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحظوظ والشهوات<\/bdi>) and reliance upon habits and familiar comforts ascends in spirit to the heaven of holiness and the abode of intimacy; there he obtains honour that does not perish, extending to his offspring and associates until God inherits the earth and its inhabitants—He is the best of inheritors. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N023\">[XIV-N023]<\/a><\/p><p>This is God’s established way with His creation. For they supported His religion and raised His Word, so He helped and raised them. “If you help God, He will help you” [47:7]. “He made the word of those who disbelieved the lowest, while the Word of God is the highest” [9:40]. In the <i>Ḥikam<\/i>: “If you desire an honour that does not perish, do not seek honour through what perishes.” God Most High knows best.<\/p><p>Al-Qushayrī says in allusion: “I shall take you away from yourself, seize you from yourself, raise you beyond human qualities, and purify you altogether of your self-will, until you attest to Us through Us. Nothing of your independent choice will remain, and the cloak of Our care shall rest upon you. It was in this state that the raising of the dead appeared at his hand; these events came about only through the Power governing him.” End of quotation. Al-Wartajbī says: “I shall take you away from the vestiges of contingent being (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رسم الحدوثية<\/bdi>), raise you to Me through the attribute of Lordship (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعت الربوبية<\/bdi>), and purify you from the admixtures of human nature.” End of quotation.<\/p><p>He then recounts Jesus’ coming to be and his creation:<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever purifies the innermost secret ( السرّ ) of turbidity, sanctifies the spirit from the impurity of otherness ( الأغيار ), and lifts his aspiration beyond this abode, God causes his spirit to ascend to the heaven of malakūt and elevates his secret to the witnessing of the splendour of jabarūt ( مشاهدة سنا الجبروت ). His renown remains alive after death, and those attached to him enjoy care, reverence, dignity and honour. The Prophet is reported to have said: “Emigrate, and you will gain honour for your children.” Whoever leaves the homeland of personal portions and passions ( الحظوظ والشهوات ) and reliance upon habits and familiar comforts ascends in spirit to the heaven of holiness and the abode of intimacy; there he obtains honour that does not perish, extending to his offspring and associates until God inherits the earth and its inhabitants—He is the best of inheritors. [XIV-N023] This is God’s established way with His creation. For they supported His religion and raised His Word, so He helped and raised them. “If you help God, He will help you” [47:7]. “He made the word of those who disbelieved the lowest, while the Word of God is the highest” [9:40]. In the Ḥikam : “If you desire an honour that does not perish, do not seek honour through what perishes.” God Most High knows best. Al-Qushayrī says in allusion: “I shall take you away from yourself, seize you from yourself, raise you beyond human qualities, and purify you altogether of your self-will, until you attest to Us through Us. Nothing of your independent choice will remain, and the cloak of Our care shall rest upon you. It was in this state that the raising of the dead appeared at his hand; these events came about only through the Power governing him.” End of quotation. Al-Wartajbī says: “I shall take you away from the vestiges of contingent being ( رسم الحدوثية ), raise you to Me through the attribute of Lordship ( نعت الربوبية ), and purify you from the admixtures of human nature.” End of quotation. He then recounts Jesus’ coming to be and his creation:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C360.1","page":360,"ar":"أي: إلى زمن المؤلف، أما في زمننا، فقد أنشأوا لهم دولة، في قلب عالمنا الإسلامي، في فلسطين العربية، بمعاونة الدول الكافرة. فاللهم أنزل دولتهم وفرق شملهم.. آمين.","en":"That is, up to the author’s time. In our own time, a state has been established for them in the heart of our Islamic world, in Arab Palestine, with the aid of the unbelieving states. O God, bring down their state and scatter their unity. Amen.","anchor":"إلى زمننا هذا"}],"english_characters":4958,"arabic_characters":3669},{"id":"q3-59-60","q":"3:59–60","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:59–60 · Jesus and Adam; the human composite and the ascent of spirit","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 361–362","cairo":"I:361–362","source_page_first":361,"source_page_last":362,"ar":"إِنَّ مَثَلَ عِيسى عِنْدَ اللَّهِ كَمَثَلِ آدَمَ خَلَقَهُ مِنْ تُرابٍ ثُمَّ قالَ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ (59) الْحَقُّ مِنْ رَبِّكَ فَلا تَكُنْ مِنَ الْمُمْتَرِينَ (60)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ مَثَلَ عِيسى عِنْدَ اللَّهِ أي: إن شأنه الغريب في كونه وجد من غير أب (كمثل آدم) . ثم فسر شأن آدم فقال: خَلَقَهُ مِنْ تُرابٍ أي: خلق قالبه من تراب، ثُمَّ نفخ فيه الروح، وقالَ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ أي: فكان، فشأنه أغرب من شأن عيسى، لأنه وجد من غير أب ولا أم، بخلاف عيسى عليه السلام، فلا يستغرب حاله ويتغالى فيه إلا من طَبع اللهُ على قلبه، فاستعجز القدرة الإلهية، وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ مُقْتَدِراً. هّذاَ هُوَ الْحَقُّ مِنْ رَبِّكَ فَلا تَكُنْ مِنَ الْمُمْتَرِينَ أي: الشاكين في مخلوقيته، وهذا خطاب للنبى صلّى الله عليه وسلم، على طريق التهييج لغيره، أو لكل سامع.\nوسبب نزول الآية: أنَّ وفد نجران قالوا للنبى صلّى الله عليه وسلم: مالك تشتم صاحبنا، فتقول: إنه عبد؟ قال: أجل، هو عبد الله ورسوله، وكلمته ألقاها إلى مريم العذراء البتول، فغضبوا، وقالوا: هل رأيت إنساناً قط من غير أب؟ فإن كنت صادقاً فأرنا مثله. فنزلت: إِنَّ مَثَلَ عِيسى عِنْدَ اللَّهِ كَمَثَلِ آدَمَ. أي: فهو أعجب من عيسى، لكونه بلا واسطة أصلاً. رُوِيَ أن مريم حملت بعيسى وهي بنت ثلاث عشرة سنة، وأوحى الله إليه على رأس ثلاثين سنة، ورفعه إليه من بيت المقدس ليلة القدر، وهو ابن ثلاث وثلاثين سنة، وعاشت أمه بعد رفعه ست سنين.\nقال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «أنا أولى الناس بعيسى ابن مريم لأنه لم يكن بيني وبينه نبيّ، فإنه نازل بأمتي وخليفتي فيهم، فإذا رأيتموه فاعرفوه، فإنه رجل مربوع إلى الحمرة والبياض، سبط الشعر، كأن شعره يقطر، وإن لم يصبه بلل، يدق الصليب، ويقتل الخنزير، ويفيض المال، وليسلكن الروحاء(1) ، حاجّاً أو معتمراً، أو ليَثْنِينَّهما جميعاً، ويقاتل الناس على الإسلام، حتى يُهلك الله في زمانه الملل كلها، ويهلك الله في زمانه مسيح الضلالة، الكذاب\nالدجال، وتقع في الأرض الأمنة، حتى ترتع الأسد مع الإبل، والنمر مع البقر، والذئاب مع الغنم، ويلعب الغلمان بالحيات، ويلبث في الأرض أربعين سنة، ثم يتزوج ويولد له ثم يتوفى، ويصلي المسلمون عليه» . ويدفنونه في حجرة النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن الحق- جلّ جلاله- أظهر هذا الآدمي في شكل غريب، وسر عجيب، جمع فيه بين الضدين، وأودع فيه سر الكونين، نوراني ظلماني، روحاني جسماني، سماوي أرضي، ملكوتي ملكي، معنوي حسيّ، أودع فيه الروح نورانية لاهوتية في نطفة ناسوتية، فوقع التنازع بين الضدين، فالروح تحن إلى وطنها اللاهوتي، والنطفة الطينية تحن إلى وطنها الناسوتي، فمن غلبت روحانيته على طينته التحق بالروحانيين، وكان من المقربين في أعلى عليين، فصارت همته منصرفة إلى طاعة مولاه، والارتقاء إلى مشاهدة نوره وسناه، فانياً عن حظوظه وهواه، ومَن غلبت طينتُه على روحانيته التحق بالبهائم أو الشياطين، وانحط إلى أسفل سافلين، وكانت همته منصرفة إلى حظوظه وهواه، غائباً عن ذكر مولاه، قد اتخذ إلهه هواه.\nوتأمل قضية السيد عيسى عليه السلام لمَّا لم ينشأ من نطفة أمشاجية، كيف غلبت روحانيته، حيث لم تجد ما يجذبها إلى الحضيض الطيني، فلم يلتفت إلى هذا العالم الظلماني أصلاً، وكذلك الأنبياء حيث طهروا من بقاياها في الأصالة، والأولياء حيث طهروها بالمجاهدة، كيف صارت أرواحهم لاتشتاق إلا إلى الأذكار والعلوم والأسرار، فانية في محبة الواحد القهار، حتى لحقت بوطنها، ورجعت إلى أصلها، محل المشاهدة والمكالمة والمناجاة والمساررة، هّذاَ هُوَ الحق مِنْ ربك فلا تكن من الممترين في إدراك الروح هذا المقام، إن لم يغلب عليها عالم الصلْصَال. والله- تعالى- أعلم.\nولما قامت الحجة على النصارى، وتبيّن عنادهم، دعاهم إلى المباهلة، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ مَثَلَ عِيسى عِنْدَ اللَّهِ كَمَثَلِ آدَمَ خَلَقَهُ مِنْ تُرابٍ ثُمَّ قالَ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ (59) الْحَقُّ مِنْ رَبِّكَ فَلا تَكُنْ مِنَ الْمُمْتَرِينَ (60)","quran_en":"59. Truly, the likeness of Jesus with God is as the likeness of Adam: He created him from dust, then said to him, “Be!” and he was. 60. The truth is from your Lord; so be not among those who doubt.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Adam without parents and Jesus without a father","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Truly, the likeness of Jesus with God”—the wonder of his having come into existence without a father—“is as the likeness of Adam.” God then explains Adam’s case: “He created him from dust,” that is, fashioned his bodily frame from earth; then He breathed the spirit into him, and said to him “Be!”—“and he is,” meaning he came to be. Adam’s case is more extraordinary than Jesus’, since Adam existed without father or mother, whereas Jesus had a mother. Thus only one whose heart God has sealed, who regards divine power as incapable, finds Jesus’ case inexplicable and exalts him beyond proper measure. “God has power over everything” [18:45]. “That is the truth from your Lord: be not among the doubters”—those who doubt that Jesus is a created being. The address is directed to the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, to stir others to heed; or to every hearer.<\/p><p>As the occasion of revelation it is related that the delegation from Najrān said to the Prophet, “Why do you insult our master by calling him a servant?” He replied: “Yes, he is God’s servant and His Messenger, His Word cast to Mary, the Virgin.” They grew angry and asked: “Have you ever seen a human being without a father? If you speak truth, show us another.” Then the verse was revealed: “The likeness of Jesus with God is as the likeness of Adam.” Adam is more remarkable, having come to be with no parental intermediary whatever. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N024\">[XIV-N024]<\/a><\/p><p>It is related that Mary conceived Jesus when she was thirteen; God revealed to him when he reached thirty, and raised him from Jerusalem on the Night of Power when he was thirty-three; his mother lived six years after his being raised. A lengthy report attributed to the Prophet says: “I am the closest of people to Jesus son of Mary, for no prophet came between him and me. He will descend among my community and act as my deputy in it. When you see him, recognize him: he is a man of medium stature, reddish and white in complexion, with straight hair, as though water dripped from it even when it was not wet. He will break the cross and kill the pig, and wealth will become abundant. He will pass through al-Rawḥāʾ as a pilgrim or performer of ʿumra, perhaps combining both. He will fight people for Islam, until God brings all other religions to an end in his time and destroys in his time the false Messiah, the lying Dajjāl. Peace will spread upon the earth, so lions will graze with camels, leopards with cattle and wolves with sheep, and children will play with snakes. He will remain on earth forty years; then he will marry, have offspring and die, and the Muslims will pray over him.” The narration adds that they will bury him in the Prophet’s chamber. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: Such statements belong to the transmitted report, and individual details require their own authentication.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N025\">[XIV-N025]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Truly, the likeness of Jesus with God”—the wonder of his having come into existence without a father—“is as the likeness of Adam.” God then explains Adam’s case: “He created him from dust,” that is, fashioned his bodily frame from earth; then He breathed the spirit into him, and said to him “Be!”—“and he is,” meaning he came to be. Adam’s case is more extraordinary than Jesus’, since Adam existed without father or mother, whereas Jesus had a mother. Thus only one whose heart God has sealed, who regards divine power as incapable, finds Jesus’ case inexplicable and exalts him beyond proper measure. “God has power over everything” [18:45]. “That is the truth from your Lord: be not among the doubters”—those who doubt that Jesus is a created being. The address is directed to the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, to stir others to heed; or to every hearer. As the occasion of revelation it is related that the delegation from Najrān said to the Prophet, “Why do you insult our master by calling him a servant?” He replied: “Yes, he is God’s servant and His Messenger, His Word cast to Mary, the Virgin.” They grew angry and asked: “Have you ever seen a human being without a father? If you speak truth, show us another.” Then the verse was revealed: “The likeness of Jesus with God is as the likeness of Adam.” Adam is more remarkable, having come to be with no parental intermediary whatever. [XIV-N024] It is related that Mary conceived Jesus when she was thirteen; God revealed to him when he reached thirty, and raised him from Jerusalem on the Night of Power when he was thirty-three; his mother lived six years after his being raised. A lengthy report attributed to the Prophet says: “I am the closest of people to Jesus son of Mary, for no prophet came between him and me. He will descend among my community and act as my deputy in it. When you see him, recognize him: he is a man of medium stature, reddish and white in complexion, with straight hair, as though water dripped from it even when it was not wet. He will break the cross and kill the pig, and wealth will become abundant. He will pass through al-Rawḥāʾ as a pilgrim or performer of ʿumra, perhaps combining both. He will fight people for Islam, until God brings all other religions to an end in his time and destroys in his time the false Messiah, the lying Dajjāl. Peace will spread upon the earth, so lions will graze with camels, leopards with cattle and wolves with sheep, and children will play with snakes. He will remain on earth forty years; then he will marry, have offspring and die, and the Muslims will pray over him.” The narration adds that they will bury him in the Prophet’s chamber. [Translator’s note: Such statements belong to the transmitted report, and individual details require their own authentication.] [XIV-N025]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Spirit and clay in the human being","html":"<p>Know that the Real, exalted is His majesty, manifested this human being in a wondrous form and with an extraordinary mystery. He joined two opposites in him and entrusted to him the mystery of two worlds: luminous and dark, spiritual and bodily, heavenly and earthly, of malakūt and of mulk, intelligible and sensible. Into a human (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ناسوتية<\/bdi>) drop He placed a luminous, divine (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لاهوتية<\/bdi>) spirit. Thus a conflict arose between the opposites: the spirit yearns for its divine home, while the clay-formed drop yearns for its human home. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N026\">[XIV-N026]<\/a><\/p><p>Whoever allows the spiritual nature to prevail over the clay joins the spiritual beings and becomes among those brought near in the highest heights. His aspiration is turned to obedience to his Master and ascent to the witnessing of His light and splendour, effaced from his personal portion and passion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فانيًا عن حظوظه وهواه<\/bdi>). Whoever lets the clay nature prevail over the spirit resembles beasts or devils and falls to the lowest depths. His aspiration remains turned towards his pleasures and passions, absent from remembrance of his Master, having taken his passion as his god.<\/p><p>Consider our master Jesus, peace upon him: because he did not originate from a mingled drop (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نطفة أمشاجية<\/bdi>), his spiritual nature prevailed; he had nothing drawing him towards the lowliness of clay, so he did not turn to this dark world at all. Likewise the prophets, who were purified of its residues from the outset, and the friends of God, who purified them through spiritual struggle (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المجاهدة<\/bdi>): their spirits desired only invocations, knowledge and mysteries, effaced in love of the One, the Overpowering, until they rejoined their home and returned to their origin—the station of witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشاهدة<\/bdi>), intimate speaking (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المكالمة<\/bdi>), supplication (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المناجاة<\/bdi>) and mutual confidence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المساررة<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>“This is the truth from your Lord; do not be among those who doubt” that the spirit can attain this station, provided the realm of clay (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الصلصال<\/bdi>) does not dominate it. And God Most High knows best.<\/p><p>After the argument against the Christians had been established and their obstinacy exposed, he invited them to a mutual invocation of judgment (<i>mubāhala<\/i>, <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المباهلة<\/bdi>), saying:<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that the Real, exalted is His majesty, manifested this human being in a wondrous form and with an extraordinary mystery. He joined two opposites in him and entrusted to him the mystery of two worlds: luminous and dark, spiritual and bodily, heavenly and earthly, of malakūt and of mulk, intelligible and sensible. Into a human ( ناسوتية ) drop He placed a luminous, divine ( لاهوتية ) spirit. Thus a conflict arose between the opposites: the spirit yearns for its divine home, while the clay-formed drop yearns for its human home. [XIV-N026] Whoever allows the spiritual nature to prevail over the clay joins the spiritual beings and becomes among those brought near in the highest heights. His aspiration is turned to obedience to his Master and ascent to the witnessing of His light and splendour, effaced from his personal portion and passion ( فانيًا عن حظوظه وهواه ). Whoever lets the clay nature prevail over the spirit resembles beasts or devils and falls to the lowest depths. His aspiration remains turned towards his pleasures and passions, absent from remembrance of his Master, having taken his passion as his god. Consider our master Jesus, peace upon him: because he did not originate from a mingled drop ( نطفة أمشاجية ), his spiritual nature prevailed; he had nothing drawing him towards the lowliness of clay, so he did not turn to this dark world at all. Likewise the prophets, who were purified of its residues from the outset, and the friends of God, who purified them through spiritual struggle ( المجاهدة ): their spirits desired only invocations, knowledge and mysteries, effaced in love of the One, the Overpowering, until they rejoined their home and returned to their origin—the station of witnessing ( المشاهدة ), intimate speaking ( المكالمة ), supplication ( المناجاة ) and mutual confidence ( المساررة ). “This is the truth from your Lord; do not be among those who doubt” that the spirit can attain this station, provided the realm of clay ( عالم الصلصال ) does not dominate it. And God Most High knows best. After the argument against the Christians had been established and their obstinacy exposed, he invited them to a mutual invocation of judgment ( mubāhala , المباهلة ), saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C361.1","page":361,"ar":"الروحاء: طريق بين مكة والمدينة، كان طريق رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم إلى بدر، وإلى مكة، عام الفتح وعام الحج.","en":"Al-Rawḥāʾ: a road between Mecca and Medina, taken by the Messenger of God, blessings and peace be upon him, on his way to Badr and to Mecca in the year of the conquest and the year of the pilgrimage.","anchor":"الروحاء"}],"english_characters":5084,"arabic_characters":3194},{"id":"q3-61-63","q":"3:61–63","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:61–63 · The challenge of mutual invocation and devotion to the spiritual master","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 362–364","cairo":"I:362–364","source_page_first":362,"source_page_last":364,"ar":"فَمَنْ حَاجَّكَ فِيهِ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَكَ مِنَ الْعِلْمِ فَقُلْ تَعالَوْا نَدْعُ أَبْناءَنا وَأَبْناءَكُمْ وَنِساءَنا وَنِساءَكُمْ وَأَنْفُسَنا وَأَنْفُسَكُمْ ثُمَّ نَبْتَهِلْ فَنَجْعَلْ لَعْنَتَ اللَّهِ عَلَى الْكاذِبِينَ (61) إِنَّ هذا لَهُوَ الْقَصَصُ الْحَقُّ وَما مِنْ إِلهٍ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَإِنَّ اللَّهَ لَهُوَ الْعَزِيزُ الْحَكِيمُ (62) فَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلِيمٌ بِالْمُفْسِدِينَ (63)\nقلت: أصل (تعالوا) : تَعالَيُوا، على وزن تفاعلوا، من العلو، فقلبت الياء ألفاً لتحركها، ثم حذفت، ومن قرأ بالضم نقل، وأصل معناها: ارتفع، ثم أطلق على الأمر بالمجيء. والابتهال: التضرّع والمبالغة في الدعاء.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فَمَنْ خاصمك يا محمد في شأن عيسى عليه السلام، وكان الذي خاصم في ذلك السيد والعاقب، لما قدموا مع نصارى نجران على النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، قال لهما النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «أسلما» ، قالا: قد أسلمنا قبلك، قال: «كذبتما، يمنعكما من الإسلام دعاؤكما عيسى لله ولداً، وعبادتكما الصليب، وأكلكما الخنزير» ، قالا: إن\nلم يكن عيسى ولداً لله فمن أبوه؟ فقال لهما النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: ألستم تعلمون أنه لا يكون ولد إلا وهو يشبه أباه؟ قالوا: بلى، قال: ألستم تعلمون أن ربنا حي لا يموت، وأن عيسى يأتي عليه الفناء؟ قالوا: بلى، قال: ألستم تعلمون أن ربنا قيِّم كل شيء، ويحفظه، ويرزقه؟ قالوا: بلى، قال: فهل ملك عيسى شيئاً من ذلك؟ فقالوا: لا. قال: ألستم تعلمون أن الله لا يخفى عليه شيء في الأرض ولا في السماء؟ قالوا: بلى، قال: فهل يعلم عيسى من ذلك إلا ما عُلِّم؟ قالوا: لا.\nقال: فإن ربنا صوَّر عيسى في الرحم كيف شاء، وربنا لا يأكل ولا يشرب ولا يُحدث، قالوا: بلى. قال: ألستم تعلمون أن عيسى حملته أمه كما تحمل المرأة ثم وضعته كما تضع المرأة ولدها، ثم غُذِّي كما يُغَذّى الصبي، ثم كان يطعم ويشرب ويحدث؟ قالوا: بلى. قال: كيف يكون هذا كما زعمتم؟ فسكتوا ... فأنزل فيهم السورة إلى هنا.\nفقال الحقّ لنبيه- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: فَمَنْ حَاجَّكَ فِيهِ أي: في عيسى من النصارى، مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَكَ مِنَ الْعِلْمِ بعبوديته، فَقُلْ لهم: تَعالَوْا نَتَلاَعَنُ، أي: نلعن الكاذبَ منا نَدْعُ أَبْناءَنا وَأَبْناءَكُمْ وَنِساءَنا وَنِساءَكُمْ وَأَنْفُسَنا وَأَنْفُسَكُمْ أي: يدعو كل واحد منا نفسه وأعزة أهله وألصقهم بقلبه إلى المباهلة، وإنما قدَّمهم على النفس لأن الرجل يخاطر بنفسه دونهم، فكان تقديمهم أبلغ في الابتهال، ثُمَّ نَبْتَهِلْ، أي نجهد في الدعاء على الكاذب، فَنَجْعَلْ لَعْنَتَ اللَّهِ عَلَى الْكاذِبِينَ.\nفلما قرأ النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم هذه الآية على وفد نجران، ودعاهم إلى المباهلة، قالوا: حتى نرجع وننظر في أمرنا، فقالوا للعاقب- وكان ذا رأيهم-: ما ترى؟ فقال: والله لقد عرفتم يا معشر النصارى أن محمداً نبيّ مرسل، ولقد جاءكم بالفَصْل من أمر صاحبكم، والله ما لاعن قومٌ قط نبيّاً فعاش كبيرُهم، ولا نبت صغيرُهم، ولئن فعلتم ذلك لتهلكن، فوادِعُوا الرَّجُلَ. وانصرفوا، فأتوه وهو محتضنٌ الحسن آخذ بيد الحسين، وفاطمةُ تمشي خلفه، وعَلِيّ خلفها، وهو يقول لهم: «إِذَا دَعَوْتُ فَأمِّنُوا» ، فقال الأسقُف: يا معشر النصارى، إني لأرى وجُوهاً لو سألوا الله أن يُزيل جبلاً من مكانه لأزاله، فلا تتباهلوا فتهلكوا جميعاً إلى يوم القيامة. فقالوا: يا أبا القاسم، نرى ألا نلاعنك، فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: أسْلِمُوا يَكُنْ لكم ما للمسلمين وعليكم ما عليهم، فأَبَوا، فقال: إنى أنابذكم، فقالوا: مالنا بحرب العرب طاقة، ولكنا نصالحك على ألا تغزونا ولا تَرُدَّنا عن ديننا، على أن نؤدي إليك في كل عام ألفَيْ حلة ألفاً في صَفَر، ألفا في رجب، وثلاثين درعاً من حديد.\nفصالحهم النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم على ذلك، فقال النبيّ: «والذي نفسي بيده لو تلاعنوا لمُسِخُوا قِرَدَة، وخَنَازِيرَ، ولأضْرَمَ عَلَيْهِمْ الوَادِي نَاراً، ولاستأصل اللهُ نَجْرَان وأهْلَهُ، ولَمَا حَالَ الحَوْلُ على النَّصَارى كلّهم حَتى هَلكوا» .\nقال الله تعالى: إِنَّ هذا الذي أوحينا إليك لَهُوَ الْقَصَصُ الْحَقُّ وَما مِنْ إِلهٍ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، خلافاً لما يزعم النصارى من التثليث، وَإِنَّ اللَّهَ لَهُوَ الْعَزِيزُ في ملكه الْحَكِيمُ في صنعه، فلا أحد يساويه في قدرته التامة، ولا في حكمته البالغة، فَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا وأعرضوا عن الإيمان، فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلِيمٌ بِالْمُفْسِدِينَ، الذين يعبدون غير الله.\nووضع المظهر موضع الضمير، ليدل على أن التولي عن الحجج والإعراض عن التوحيد إفساد للدين، بل يؤدي إلى فساد العالم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: ينبغي للمريد، الذي تحقق بخصوصية شيخه، أن يلاعن من يخاصمه فيه، ويبعد عنه كل البعد، ولا يلين له لئلا يركبه، ويدفع عن شيخه ما استطاع، فإنَّ هذا من التعظيم الذي هو سببٌ في سعادة المريد، ولا يصغي إلى المفسدين الطاعنين في أنصار الدين. قلت: وقد جاءني بعض من ينتسب إلى العلم من أهل فاس، فقال لي: قد اتفقت علماء فاس على بدعة شيخكم، فقلت له: لو اتفق أهل السموات السبع والأرضين السبع، على أنه من أهل البدعة، لقلت أنا: إنه من أهل السنّة، لأني تحققت بخصوصيته، كالشمس في أفق السماء، ليس دونها سحاب. فالله يرزقنا حسن الأدب معهم والتعظيم إلى يوم الدين. آمين. فمن أعرض عن أولياء الله من المنكرين (فإن الله عليم بالمفسدين) .\nثم دعاهم إلى التوحيد الذي اتفقت عليه سائر الأديان، فقال:","quran_ar":"فَمَنْ حَاجَّكَ فِيهِ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَكَ مِنَ الْعِلْمِ فَقُلْ تَعالَوْا نَدْعُ أَبْناءَنا وَأَبْناءَكُمْ وَنِساءَنا وَنِساءَكُمْ وَأَنْفُسَنا وَأَنْفُسَكُمْ ثُمَّ نَبْتَهِلْ فَنَجْعَلْ لَعْنَتَ اللَّهِ عَلَى الْكاذِبِينَ (61) إِنَّ هذا لَهُوَ الْقَصَصُ الْحَقُّ وَما مِنْ إِلهٍ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَإِنَّ اللَّهَ لَهُوَ الْعَزِيزُ الْحَكِيمُ (62) فَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلِيمٌ بِالْمُفْسِدِينَ (63)","quran_en":"61. Whoever disputes with you concerning him after knowledge has come to you, say: “Come, let us call our sons and your sons, our women and your women, ourselves and yourselves; then let us earnestly invoke [God] and call God’s curse upon the liars.” 62. This is surely the true account. There is no deity save God, and God is truly the Mighty, the Wise. 63. If they turn away, God knows those who spread corruption.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The derivation of “come” and the meaning of mubāhala","html":"<p>I say: the original of “Come!” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تعالَوا<\/bdi>, <i>taʿālaw<\/i>) is <i>taʿālayū<\/i>, on the pattern <i>tafāʿalū<\/i>, from <i>ʿuluww<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلوّ<\/bdi>), height or elevation. The yāʾ changed to alif because it was vocalized; the alif was subsequently elided. In the reading with ḍamma, the vowel has been transferred [from the yāʾ to the lām]. Its original meaning was “rise up,” after which it became a general imperative meaning “come.” Mutual invocation (<i>ibtihāl<\/i>, <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الابتهال<\/bdi>) means humbling oneself intensely and striving earnestly in prayer.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the original of “Come!” ( تعالَوا , taʿālaw ) is taʿālayū , on the pattern tafāʿalū , from ʿuluww ( العلوّ ), height or elevation. The yāʾ changed to alif because it was vocalized; the alif was subsequently elided. In the reading with ḍamma, the vowel has been transferred [from the yāʾ to the lām]. Its original meaning was “rise up,” after which it became a general imperative meaning “come.” Mutual invocation ( ibtihāl , الابتهال ) means humbling oneself intensely and striving earnestly in prayer."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Najrān and the challenge of mubāhala","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Whoever disputes with you,” Muḥammad, “concerning him,” Jesus, after the knowledge concerning his servanthood has come to you ... Those who disputed are said to have been al-Sayyid and al-ʿĀqib, who came with the Christians of Najrān. The Prophet said to them, “Submit [become Muslims].” They answered, “We submitted before you.” He replied: “You speak falsely: your calling Jesus God’s son, worshipping the cross and eating pork prevents your submission.” They asked, “If Jesus is not God’s son, who is his father?”<\/p><p>The Prophet then asked whether a child does not resemble its father; whether God is living and never dies, while Jesus is subject to passing away; whether God sustains, protects and provides for all things, while Jesus cannot exercise such sovereignty; and whether nothing in earth or heaven escapes God’s knowledge, whereas Jesus knows only what he has been taught. They acknowledged each point. He continued: “God formed Jesus in the womb as He willed. God neither eats, drinks nor expels waste. Was Jesus not borne by his mother, delivered as other children are, fed and nourished, and himself subject to eating, drinking and the needs of the body?” When they assented, he asked how Jesus could be as they claimed. They fell silent. The narrative says that the opening of this sūrah was then revealed down to the present passage. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N027\">[XIV-N027]<\/a><\/p><p>Thus God said to His Prophet: Whoever disputes with you over Jesus, “after knowledge has come to you,” say to them: “Come, let us invoke a curse upon the liar among us. Let us call our sons and your sons, our women and your women, ourselves and yourselves”—that is, each party should bring itself and the dearest members of its family, those most closely bound to its heart, for the <i>mubāhala<\/i>. They are named before the individual himself because a man will hazard his own safety sooner than theirs, making the summons a more forceful test. “Then let us earnestly pray” against the party who lies, “and place God’s curse upon the liars.”<\/p><p>When the Prophet recited the verse and invited the delegation to mutual invocation, they requested time to consult. They asked al-ʿĀqib, their adviser, what he thought. He said: “You know that Muḥammad is a messenger, and that he has brought a decisive judgment concerning your master. No people who entered into mutual imprecation with a prophet ever had their elders survive or their children grow up. If you do so, you will perish; make peace with the man.” They then came to him while he held al-Ḥasan close, took al-Ḥusayn by the hand, and walked with Fāṭima behind him and ʿAlī behind her, telling them, “When I invoke, say ‘Amen.’” Their bishop is reported to have warned that these were faces whose prayer could move a mountain and that the delegation should not invoke destruction upon themselves. They declined the <i>mubāhala<\/i>.<\/p><p>The Prophet invited them again to enter Islam and receive the rights and responsibilities of Muslims. When they refused, he said that he would oppose them. They answered that they could not wage war against the Arabs, but sought terms by which he would not attack them or compel them to leave their religion. The reported settlement required two thousand garments each year, one thousand in Ṣafar and one thousand in Rajab, together with thirty iron coats of mail. The narrative adds the Prophet’s warning that, had they undertaken <i>mubāhala<\/i>, they would have been transformed into monkeys and pigs, their valley consumed in fire, Najrān and its people uprooted, and no year would have elapsed before all the Christians perished. <span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: All these details are presented as reports in the source, not as independent archival proof of the encounter or treaty.]<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N028\">[XIV-N028]<\/a><\/p><p>The Real says: “This” account revealed to you “is the true narration. There is no deity but God,” contrary to the Christian trinitarian claim as Ibn ʿAjība construes it. “God is the Mighty” in His kingship, “the Wise” in His creation. None equals Him in perfect power or consummate wisdom. “If they turn away” from faith, “God knows the corrupters,” those who worship other than Him. The explicit noun “the corrupters” stands in place of a pronoun to indicate that turning away from proofs and from divine unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد<\/bdi>) corrupts religion and leads to disorder in the world. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Whoever disputes with you,” Muḥammad, “concerning him,” Jesus, after the knowledge concerning his servanthood has come to you ... Those who disputed are said to have been al-Sayyid and al-ʿĀqib, who came with the Christians of Najrān. The Prophet said to them, “Submit [become Muslims].” They answered, “We submitted before you.” He replied: “You speak falsely: your calling Jesus God’s son, worshipping the cross and eating pork prevents your submission.” They asked, “If Jesus is not God’s son, who is his father?” The Prophet then asked whether a child does not resemble its father; whether God is living and never dies, while Jesus is subject to passing away; whether God sustains, protects and provides for all things, while Jesus cannot exercise such sovereignty; and whether nothing in earth or heaven escapes God’s knowledge, whereas Jesus knows only what he has been taught. They acknowledged each point. He continued: “God formed Jesus in the womb as He willed. God neither eats, drinks nor expels waste. Was Jesus not borne by his mother, delivered as other children are, fed and nourished, and himself subject to eating, drinking and the needs of the body?” When they assented, he asked how Jesus could be as they claimed. They fell silent. The narrative says that the opening of this sūrah was then revealed down to the present passage. [XIV-N027] Thus God said to His Prophet: Whoever disputes with you over Jesus, “after knowledge has come to you,” say to them: “Come, let us invoke a curse upon the liar among us. Let us call our sons and your sons, our women and your women, ourselves and yourselves”—that is, each party should bring itself and the dearest members of its family, those most closely bound to its heart, for the mubāhala . They are named before the individual himself because a man will hazard his own safety sooner than theirs, making the summons a more forceful test. “Then let us earnestly pray” against the party who lies, “and place God’s curse upon the liars.” When the Prophet recited the verse and invited the delegation to mutual invocation, they requested time to consult. They asked al-ʿĀqib, their adviser, what he thought. He said: “You know that Muḥammad is a messenger, and that he has brought a decisive judgment concerning your master. No people who entered into mutual imprecation with a prophet ever had their elders survive or their children grow up. If you do so, you will perish; make peace with the man.” They then came to him while he held al-Ḥasan close, took al-Ḥusayn by the hand, and walked with Fāṭima behind him and ʿAlī behind her, telling them, “When I invoke, say ‘Amen.’” Their bishop is reported to have warned that these were faces whose prayer could move a mountain and that the delegation should not invoke destruction upon themselves. They declined the mubāhala . The Prophet invited them again to enter Islam and receive the rights and responsibilities of Muslims. When they refused, he said that he would oppose them. They answered that they could not wage war against the Arabs, but sought terms by which he would not attack them or compel them to leave their religion. The reported settlement required two thousand garments each year, one thousand in Ṣafar and one thousand in Rajab, together with thirty iron coats of mail. The narrative adds the Prophet’s warning that, had they undertaken mubāhala , they would have been transformed into monkeys and pigs, their valley consumed in fire, Najrān and its people uprooted, and no year would have elapsed before all the Christians perished. [Translator’s note: All these details are presented as reports in the source, not as independent archival proof of the encounter or treaty.] [XIV-N028] The Real says: “This” account revealed to you “is the true narration. There is no deity but God,” contrary to the Christian trinitarian claim as Ibn ʿAjība construes it. “God is the Mighty” in His kingship, “the Wise” in His creation. None equals Him in perfect power or consummate wisdom. “If they turn away” from faith, “God knows the corrupters,” those who worship other than Him. The explicit noun “the corrupters” stands in place of a pronoun to indicate that turning away from proofs and from divine unity ( التوحيد ) corrupts religion and leads to disorder in the world. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Loyalty to one’s shaykh and the trial of denial","html":"<p>The aspirant (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المريد<\/bdi>) who has become certain of his shaykh’s particular spiritual distinction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خصوصية شيخه<\/bdi>), Ibn ʿAjība says, should engage in mutual imprecation with anyone who disputes with him about that shaykh, keep his distance from them, and not soften towards them, lest they gain mastery over him. He should defend his shaykh to the extent of his ability; this reverence is a cause of happiness upon the Path. He should not listen to those who disparage the helpers of religion.<\/p><p>I say: Someone associated with learning in Fez came to me and said, “The scholars of Fez have agreed that your shaykh is an innovator.” I replied: “If all the inhabitants of the seven heavens and the seven earths agreed that he was an innovator, I would still say he belonged to the Sunna. For I have realized his particular spiritual distinction as clearly as the sun in the sky, unobscured by any cloud.” May God grant us excellent comportment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حسن الأدب<\/bdi>) towards them and reverence until the Day of Judgment. Amen. Whoever of the deniers turns away from God’s friends: “God knows the corrupters.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N029\">[XIV-N029]<\/a><\/p><p>He then calls them to the divine unity on which the religious traditions agree, saying:<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The aspirant ( المريد ) who has become certain of his shaykh’s particular spiritual distinction ( خصوصية شيخه ), Ibn ʿAjība says, should engage in mutual imprecation with anyone who disputes with him about that shaykh, keep his distance from them, and not soften towards them, lest they gain mastery over him. He should defend his shaykh to the extent of his ability; this reverence is a cause of happiness upon the Path. He should not listen to those who disparage the helpers of religion. I say: Someone associated with learning in Fez came to me and said, “The scholars of Fez have agreed that your shaykh is an innovator.” I replied: “If all the inhabitants of the seven heavens and the seven earths agreed that he was an innovator, I would still say he belonged to the Sunna. For I have realized his particular spiritual distinction as clearly as the sun in the sky, unobscured by any cloud.” May God grant us excellent comportment ( حسن الأدب ) towards them and reverence until the Day of Judgment. Amen. Whoever of the deniers turns away from God’s friends: “God knows the corrupters.” [XIV-N029] He then calls them to the divine unity on which the religious traditions agree, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":6064,"arabic_characters":4551},{"id":"q3-64","q":"3:64","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:64 · A common word; many paths and the single aim of realization","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 364–365","cairo":"I:364–365","source_page_first":364,"source_page_last":365,"ar":"قُلْ يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ تَعالَوْا إِلى كَلِمَةٍ سَواءٍ بَيْنَنا وَبَيْنَكُمْ أَلاَّ نَعْبُدَ إِلاَّ اللَّهَ وَلا نُشْرِكَ بِهِ شَيْئاً وَلا يَتَّخِذَ بَعْضُنا بَعْضاً أَرْباباً مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ فَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا فَقُولُوا اشْهَدُوا بِأَنَّا مُسْلِمُونَ (64)\nقلت: (سواء) : مصدر، نعت للكلمة، والمصادر لا تثني ولا تجمع ولا تؤنث، فإذا فتحت السين مددت، وإذا ضمت أو كسرت قصرت، كقوله: مَكاناً سُوىً أي: مستوٍ. وسواء كل شيء: وسطه، قال تعالى: فَرَآهُ فِي سَواءِ الْجَحِيمِ، أي: وسطه.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قُلْ يا محمد: يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ اليهود والنصارى، تَعالَوْا: هلموا إِلى كَلِمَةٍ سَواءٍ أي: عدل مستوية، بَيْنَنا وَبَيْنَكُمْ لا يختلف فيها الرسل والكتب والأمم، هي أَلَّا نَعْبُدَ إِلَّا اللَّهَ أي: نوحده بالعبادة، ونقر له بالوحدانية، وَلا نُشْرِكَ بِهِ شَيْئاً أي: لا نجعل غيره شريكاً له في استحقاق العبادة، وَلا يَتَّخِذَ بَعْضُنا بَعْضاً أَرْباباً مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ أي: لا نقول عزير ابن الله، ولا المسيح ابن الله، ولا نطيع الأحبار فيما أحدثوا من التحريم والتحليل، لأنهم بشر مثلنا.\nولمّا نزل قوله تعالى: اتَّخَذُوا أَحْبارَهُمْ وَرُهْبانَهُمْ أَرْباباً مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ ... قال عدي بن حاتم: ما كُنَّا نعبدهم يا رسول الله، قال: «أَلّيْس كانُوا يُحِلُّون لَكُمْ ويُحرِّمون، فتأخُذُون بقَوْلِهم؟ قال: بلى، قال: هُوَ ذَاكَ» فَإِنْ\nتَوَلَّوْا وأعرضوا عن التوحيد فَقُولُوا اشْهَدُوا بِأَنَّا مُسْلِمُونَ، فقد لزمتكم الحجة، فاعترفوا بأنا مسلمون دونكم، وأنتم كافرون بما نطقت به الكتبُ وتواطأت عليه الرسل.\nتنبيه: انظر ما في هذه الآية من المبالغة وحسن التدرج في الاحتجاج، بيِّن أولاً أحوال عيسى وما تطاور عليه من الأطوار المنافية للألوهية، ثم ذكر ما يحل عقدتهم ويزيح شبهتهم، فلما رأى عنادهم ولجاجهم دعاهم إلى المباهلة بنوع من الإعجاز، ثم لمّا أعرضوا عنها وانقادوا بعض الانقياد، عاد عليهم بالإرشاد، وسلك طريقاً أسهل وألزم، بأن دعاهم إلى ما وافق عليه عيسى وسائر الأنبياء والكتب، ثم لمّا لم يُجد ذلك فيهم شيئاً، وعلم أن الآيات والنذر لا تغني عنهم شيئاً أعرض عنهم، وقال: فَقُولُوا اشْهَدُوا بِأَنَّا مُسْلِمُونَ. قاله البيضاوي.\nالإشارة: الطرق كثيرة والمقصد واحد، وهو التوحيد الخاص، أعني مقام الفناء والبقاء. فالداعُون إلى الله كلهم متفقون على الدعوة إلى هذا المقصد، فكل طريق لا توصل إلى هذا المقصد لا عبرة بها، وكل داع لا يُبلغ إلى هذا الجمال فهو دجال، فإنْ رضي بتعظيم الناس، ولم يَبْنِ طريقه على الأساس، فليس لصاحبه إلا الإفلاس، وكل مَن أطاع المخلوق في معصية الله فقد اتخذه ربّاً من دون الله، وكل مَن تولى عن طريق الإرشاد فقد استوجب لنفسه الطرد والبعاد، فيقول له الواصلون أو السائرون: (فإن تولوا فقولوا اشهدوا بأنا مسلمون) . وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nولما قدم وفدُ نجران المدينة، التقوا مع اليهود، فاختصموا في إبراهيم عليه السّلام فأتاهم النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، فقالوا: يا محمد إنا اختلفنا فى إبراهيم ودينه، فقالت النصارى: كان نصرانيّاً، وقالت اليهود: كان يهوديّاً، وهم أولى الناس به، فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «كلا الفريقين برئ من إبراهيم، بل كان إبراهيم حنيفاً مسلماً، وأنا على دينه، فاتبعوا دينه الإسلام» . فأنزل الله:","quran_ar":"قُلْ يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ تَعالَوْا إِلى كَلِمَةٍ سَواءٍ بَيْنَنا وَبَيْنَكُمْ أَلاَّ نَعْبُدَ إِلاَّ اللَّهَ وَلا نُشْرِكَ بِهِ شَيْئاً وَلا يَتَّخِذَ بَعْضُنا بَعْضاً أَرْباباً مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ فَإِنْ تَوَلَّوْا فَقُولُوا اشْهَدُوا بِأَنَّا مُسْلِمُونَ (64)","quran_en":"64. Say: “O People of the Book, come to a word equitable between us and you: that we worship none but God, associate nothing with Him, and do not take one another as lords apart from God.” If they turn away, say: “Bear witness that we submit to God.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Sawāʾ: equity, levelness and the middle","html":"<p>I say: “Equitable” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سواء<\/bdi>) is a verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدر<\/bdi>) used as an adjective (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعت<\/bdi>) qualifying “word.” Verbal nouns [used in this way] are not put into the dual or plural, nor made feminine. With an a-vowel on the sīn, its ending is long: sawāʾ; with a u- or i-vowel, it is short: suwā or siwā, as in “a level place” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مَكَانًا سُوًى<\/bdi>) [20:58], meaning an even place. The sawāʾ of anything is its middle. God Most High says, “He saw him in the midst of Hell” [37:55], that is, in its middle.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Equitable” ( سواء ) is a verbal noun ( مصدر ) used as an adjective ( نعت ) qualifying “word.” Verbal nouns [used in this way] are not put into the dual or plural, nor made feminine. With an a-vowel on the sīn, its ending is long: sawāʾ; with a u- or i-vowel, it is short: suwā or siwā, as in “a level place” ( مَكَانًا سُوًى ) [20:58], meaning an even place. The sawāʾ of anything is its middle. God Most High says, “He saw him in the midst of Hell” [37:55], that is, in its middle."},{"type":"theology","title":"Worship, religious authority and the call to a common word","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Say,” Muḥammad, “O People of the Book”—Jews and Christians—“come,” that is, approach, “to a word equitable,” just and even-handed, “between us and you,” concerning which messengers, scriptures and communities do not differ. It is “that we worship none but God”: that we single Him out in worship and acknowledge His oneness; “associate nothing with Him”: assign no other being a share in the right to be worshipped; “and do not take one another as lords apart from God”: do not say that Ezra is God’s son or that the Messiah is God’s son, and do not obey the rabbis in the prohibitions and permissions they have newly introduced, for they are human beings like ourselves.<\/p><p>When the words of God Most High, “They have taken their rabbis and monks as lords apart from God” [9:31], were revealed, ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim said: “We did not worship them, Messenger of God.” He replied: “Did they not declare things lawful and unlawful for you, and you accepted what they said?” He answered: “Yes.” He said: “That is it.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N030\">[XIV-N030]<\/a><\/p><p>“If they turn away,” turning aside from the affirmation of God’s oneness, “say: ‘Bear witness that we submit to God.’” The proof has now become binding upon you; acknowledge, therefore, that we submit and you do not, and that you disbelieve what the scriptures have declared and the messengers have unanimously affirmed.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Say,” Muḥammad, “O People of the Book”—Jews and Christians—“come,” that is, approach, “to a word equitable,” just and even-handed, “between us and you,” concerning which messengers, scriptures and communities do not differ. It is “that we worship none but God”: that we single Him out in worship and acknowledge His oneness; “associate nothing with Him”: assign no other being a share in the right to be worshipped; “and do not take one another as lords apart from God”: do not say that Ezra is God’s son or that the Messiah is God’s son, and do not obey the rabbis in the prohibitions and permissions they have newly introduced, for they are human beings like ourselves. When the words of God Most High, “They have taken their rabbis and monks as lords apart from God” [9:31], were revealed, ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim said: “We did not worship them, Messenger of God.” He replied: “Did they not declare things lawful and unlawful for you, and you accepted what they said?” He answered: “Yes.” He said: “That is it.” [XIV-N030] “If they turn away,” turning aside from the affirmation of God’s oneness, “say: ‘Bear witness that we submit to God.’” The proof has now become binding upon you; acknowledge, therefore, that we submit and you do not, and that you disbelieve what the scriptures have declared and the messengers have unanimously affirmed."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The successive stages of the argument","html":"<p>Admonition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تنبيه<\/bdi>): consider the force and skilful progression of the argument in this verse. First, He explained Jesus’ circumstances and the successive conditions he underwent that are incompatible with divinity. Then He set out what would unravel their difficulty and remove their doubt. When He saw their obstinate insistence, He invited them to the mutual invocation of a curse (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المباهلة<\/bdi>), involving a kind of miraculous challenge. When they declined it and yielded to some extent, He returned to guiding them and took an easier, more compelling route: He invited them to that upon which Jesus, the other prophets and the scriptures agreed. When this too availed them nothing, and He knew that the signs and warnings would not benefit them, He turned away from them, saying: “Say: ‘Bear witness that we submit to God.’” Thus al-Bayḍāwī.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Admonition ( تنبيه ): consider the force and skilful progression of the argument in this verse. First, He explained Jesus’ circumstances and the successive conditions he underwent that are incompatible with divinity. Then He set out what would unravel their difficulty and remove their doubt. When He saw their obstinate insistence, He invited them to the mutual invocation of a curse ( المباهلة ), involving a kind of miraculous challenge. When they declined it and yielded to some extent, He returned to guiding them and took an easier, more compelling route: He invited them to that upon which Jesus, the other prophets and the scriptures agreed. When this too availed them nothing, and He knew that the signs and warnings would not benefit them, He turned away from them, saying: “Say: ‘Bear witness that we submit to God.’” Thus al-Bayḍāwī."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The paths are many; their aim is one","html":"<p>The paths (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطرق<\/bdi>) are many, but the aim is one: the special realization of divine oneness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد الخاص<\/bdi>), by which I mean the station of effacement and subsistence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفناء والبقاء<\/bdi>). All who call to God agree in calling to this aim. A path that does not lead to it is of no account, and every caller who fails to bring one to this beauty is an impostor. If he is content to receive people’s veneration but has not built his path upon its foundation, his companion gains nothing but bankruptcy. Whoever obeys a created being in disobedience to God has taken that being as a lord apart from God. Whoever turns from the path of right guidance has brought expulsion and remoteness upon himself. Those who have arrived, or those still journeying, say to him: “If they turn away, say: ‘Bear witness that we submit to God.’” Success comes through God, and He guides to the even way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The paths ( الطرق ) are many, but the aim is one: the special realization of divine oneness ( التوحيد الخاص ), by which I mean the station of effacement and subsistence ( الفناء والبقاء ). All who call to God agree in calling to this aim. A path that does not lead to it is of no account, and every caller who fails to bring one to this beauty is an impostor. If he is content to receive people’s veneration but has not built his path upon its foundation, his companion gains nothing but bankruptcy. Whoever obeys a created being in disobedience to God has taken that being as a lord apart from God. Whoever turns from the path of right guidance has brought expulsion and remoteness upon himself. Those who have arrived, or those still journeying, say to him: “If they turn away, say: ‘Bear witness that we submit to God.’” Success comes through God, and He guides to the even way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When the delegation from Najrān came to Medina, they met the Jews and disputed about Abraham, peace be upon him. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, came to them. They said: “Muḥammad, we have disagreed about Abraham and his religion.” The Christians said he was Christian; the Jews said he was Jewish, and that they had the closest claim to him. The Prophet said: “Both parties are disassociated from Abraham. Rather, Abraham was a ḥanīf, one submitting to God, and I follow his religion. Follow, then, his religion: submission to God.” So God revealed: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N031\">[XIV-N031]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When the delegation from Najrān came to Medina, they met the Jews and disputed about Abraham, peace be upon him. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, came to them. They said: “Muḥammad, we have disagreed about Abraham and his religion.” The Christians said he was Christian; the Jews said he was Jewish, and that they had the closest claim to him. The Prophet said: “Both parties are disassociated from Abraham. Rather, Abraham was a ḥanīf, one submitting to God, and I follow his religion. Follow, then, his religion: submission to God.” So God revealed: [XIV-N031]"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4169,"arabic_characters":2838},{"id":"q3-65-68","q":"3:65–68","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:65–68 · Abraham, submission and the limits of partisan claims","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 365–367","cairo":"I:365–367","source_page_first":365,"source_page_last":367,"ar":"يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تُحَاجُّونَ فِي إِبْراهِيمَ وَما أُنْزِلَتِ التَّوْراةُ وَالْإِنْجِيلُ إِلاَّ مِنْ بَعْدِهِ أَفَلا تَعْقِلُونَ (65) ها أَنْتُمْ هؤُلاءِ حاجَجْتُمْ فِيما لَكُمْ بِهِ عِلْمٌ فَلِمَ تُحَاجُّونَ فِيما لَيْسَ لَكُمْ بِهِ عِلْمٌ وَاللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ وَأَنْتُمْ لا تَعْلَمُونَ (66) ما كانَ إِبْراهِيمُ يَهُودِيًّا وَلا نَصْرانِيًّا وَلكِنْ كانَ حَنِيفاً مُسْلِماً وَما كانَ مِنَ الْمُشْرِكِينَ (67) إِنَّ أَوْلَى النَّاسِ بِإِبْراهِيمَ لَلَّذِينَ اتَّبَعُوهُ وَهذَا النَّبِيُّ وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَاللَّهُ وَلِيُّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ (68)\nقلت: (ها أنتم) : أصله: أنتم، دخلت عليه هاء التنبيه، وقال الأخفش: أصله: أأنتم، فقلبت الهمزة الأولى هاء، كقوله: هرقت. وتوجيه القراءات معلوم في محله، و (أنتم) : مبتدأ، و (هؤلاء) : خبره، و (حاججتم) : جملة مبينة للأولى، أو (حاججتم) : خبر، و (هؤلاء) : منادى بحذف النداء، و (حنيفاً) : حال، أي: مائلاً عن الأديان إلا دين الإسلام.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تُحَاجُّونَ فِي إِبْراهِيمَ، ويدعي كل فريق أنه كان على دينه، وَما أُنْزِلَتِ التَّوْراةُ وَالْإِنْجِيلُ إِلَّا مِنْ بَعْدِهِ، فكيف يكون يهوديّاً، ودينكم إنما حدث بعد إبراهيم بألف سنة؟! وكيف يكون نصرانيّاً، ودين النصرانية إنما ظهر بعد إبراهيم بألفي سنة؟! أَفَلا تَعْقِلُونَ فتدعون المحال، ها أَنْتُمْ يا هؤُلاءِ الحمقى حاجَجْتُمْ فِيما لَكُمْ بِهِ عِلْمٌ من أمر محمد- عليه الصلاة والسلام- ونبوته، مما وجدتموه في التوراة والإنجيل، فأنكرتموه عناداً وحسداً، فَلِمَ تجادلون فيما لا علم لكم به، ولا ذكر في كتابكم من شأن إبراهيم؟ وَاللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ ما خاصمتم فيه، وَأَنْتُمْ لا تَعْلَمُونَ، بل أنتم جاهلون.\nثم صرّح بتكذيب الفريقين فقال: ما كانَ إِبْراهِيمُ يَهُودِيًّا وَلا نَصْرانِيًّا وَلكِنْ كانَ حَنِيفاً مائلاً عن العقائد الزائفة، (مسلماً) منقاداً لأحكام ربه. وليس المراد أنه كان على ملة الإسلام، وإلا لكان مشترك الإلزام، لأن دين الإسلام مؤخر أيضاً، فكان إبراهيم إمام الموحدين، وَما كانَ مِنَ الْمُشْرِكِينَ كما عليه اليهود والنصارى والمشركون. ففيه تعريض بهم، ورد لادعائهم أنهم على ملته.\nثم ذكر مَنْ أولى الناس به، فقال: إِنَّ أَوْلَى النَّاسِ بِإِبْراهِيمَ أي: أخصهم به وأقربهم منه، لَلَّذِينَ اتَّبَعُوهُ من أمته في زمانه، وَهذَا النَّبِيُّ محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم، وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لموافقتهم له في أكثر الأحكام، قال صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «لكُلِّ نَبيَ وُلاة مِنَ النَّبيِّينَ، وإنَّ وَلِيِّي منهم أبِي وَخَلِيل ربِّي» . يعني: إبراهيم عليه السلام، وَاللَّهُ وَلِيُّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ أي: ناصرهم على سائر الأديان، ومجازيهم بغاية الإحسان.\nالإشارة: ترى كثيراً من المتفقرة يخصون الكمال بطريقهم، ويخاصمون في طريق غيرهم، وهي نزعة أهل الكتاب، حائدة عن الرشد والصواب، فأولى بالحق من اتبع السنة المحمدية، وتخلق بالأخلاق المرضية، وزهد في الدارين، ورفع همته عن الكونين، ورفع حجاب الغفلة عن قلبه، حتى أشرقت عليه أنوار ربه، واتصل بأهل التربية النبوية، فزجوا به في بحار الأحدية، ثم ردوه إلى مقام الصحو والتكميل، فياله من مقام جليل، فهذه ملة إبراهيم الخليل، وبها جاء الرسول الجليل حبيب الرحمن، وقطب دائرة الزمان، سيد المرسلين، وإمام العارفين، ورسول رب العالمين، صلى الله عليه وسلم دائماً إلى يوم الدين.\nثم شرع فى معاتبة اليهود وذكر مساوئهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تُحَاجُّونَ فِي إِبْراهِيمَ وَما أُنْزِلَتِ التَّوْراةُ وَالْإِنْجِيلُ إِلاَّ مِنْ بَعْدِهِ أَفَلا تَعْقِلُونَ (65) ها أَنْتُمْ هؤُلاءِ حاجَجْتُمْ فِيما لَكُمْ بِهِ عِلْمٌ فَلِمَ تُحَاجُّونَ فِيما لَيْسَ لَكُمْ بِهِ عِلْمٌ وَاللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ وَأَنْتُمْ لا تَعْلَمُونَ (66) ما كانَ إِبْراهِيمُ يَهُودِيًّا وَلا نَصْرانِيًّا وَلكِنْ كانَ حَنِيفاً مُسْلِماً وَما كانَ مِنَ الْمُشْرِكِينَ (67) إِنَّ أَوْلَى النَّاسِ بِإِبْراهِيمَ لَلَّذِينَ اتَّبَعُوهُ وَهذَا النَّبِيُّ وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَاللَّهُ وَلِيُّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ (68)","quran_en":"65. O People of the Book, why do you dispute about Abraham, when the Torah and the Gospel were sent down only after him? Will you not reason? 66. There you are: you have disputed about matters of which you have knowledge. Why, then, do you dispute about matters of which you have no knowledge? God knows, and you do not know. 67. Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian, but a ḥanīf, one submitting to God; he was not one of those who associate partners with Him. 68. Those with the closest claim to Abraham are surely those who followed him, this Prophet, and those who believe. God is the protecting friend of the believers.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The particles and predicates in “there you are”","html":"<p>I say: the underlying form of hā antum (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ها أنتم<\/bdi>) is antum, to which the attention-calling hāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هاء التنبيه<\/bdi>) has been prefixed. Al-Akhfash says its underlying form is a-antum (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أأنتم<\/bdi>), with the first hamza changed into hāʾ, as in haraqtu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هرقت<\/bdi>). The explanations of the variant readings are known in their proper place. Antum is the subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبتدأ<\/bdi>), hāʾulāʾ its predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>), and ḥājajtum, “you disputed,” a clause explaining the first. Alternatively, ḥājajtum is the predicate and hāʾulāʾ is a vocative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">منادى<\/bdi>) with the particle of address omitted. “A ḥanīf” is a circumstantial accusative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>), meaning one who turns away from all religions except the religion of submission to God.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the underlying form of hā antum ( ها أنتم ) is antum, to which the attention-calling hāʾ ( هاء التنبيه ) has been prefixed. Al-Akhfash says its underlying form is a-antum ( أأنتم ), with the first hamza changed into hāʾ, as in haraqtu ( هرقت ). The explanations of the variant readings are known in their proper place. Antum is the subject ( مبتدأ ), hāʾulāʾ its predicate ( خبر ), and ḥājajtum, “you disputed,” a clause explaining the first. Alternatively, ḥājajtum is the predicate and hāʾulāʾ is a vocative ( منادى ) with the particle of address omitted. “A ḥanīf” is a circumstantial accusative ( حال ), meaning one who turns away from all religions except the religion of submission to God."},{"type":"theology","title":"Abraham precedes the later communal designations","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O People of the Book, why do you dispute about Abraham,” each party claiming that he followed its religion, “when the Torah and the Gospel were sent down only after him?” How could he have been Jewish, when your religion arose only a thousand years after Abraham? How could he have been Christian, when Christianity appeared only two thousand years after Abraham? “Will you not reason,” instead of asserting what is impossible? <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N032\">[XIV-N032]<\/a><\/p><p>“There you are,” you foolish people: “you have disputed about matters of which you have knowledge”—Muḥammad’s case and his prophethood, blessings and peace be upon him, which you found in the Torah and the Gospel, but denied out of obstinacy and envy. Why, then, do you dispute about something you do not know, concerning Abraham, for which your scripture contains no statement? “God knows” the matter you dispute, “and you do not know”; rather, you are ignorant.<\/p><p>He then expressly declares both parties’ claims false: “Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian, but a ḥanīf,” turning away from false beliefs, “one submitting to God,” obedient to his Lord’s ordinances. It does not mean that he belonged to the [later] religious community of Islam (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ملّة الإسلام<\/bdi>); otherwise the same objection would apply, since the religion of Islam also came later. Abraham was the imam of those who affirm the One. “He was not one of those who associate partners with Him,” as do the Jews, Christians and polytheists. This indirectly rebukes them and refutes their claim to follow his religious way. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N033\">[XIV-N033]<\/a><\/p><p>He then states who has the closest claim to him: “Those with the closest claim to Abraham”—those most particularly related and nearest to him—“are surely those who followed him,” of his community in his own time, “this Prophet,” Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, “and those who believe,” since they agree with him in most ordinances. The Prophet said: “Every prophet has protecting friends among the prophets, and my protecting friend among them is my father and my Lord’s intimate friend,” meaning Abraham, peace be upon him. “God is the protecting friend of the believers”: He supports them against the other religions and recompenses them with the utmost beneficence.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O People of the Book, why do you dispute about Abraham,” each party claiming that he followed its religion, “when the Torah and the Gospel were sent down only after him?” How could he have been Jewish, when your religion arose only a thousand years after Abraham? How could he have been Christian, when Christianity appeared only two thousand years after Abraham? “Will you not reason,” instead of asserting what is impossible? [XIV-N032] “There you are,” you foolish people: “you have disputed about matters of which you have knowledge”—Muḥammad’s case and his prophethood, blessings and peace be upon him, which you found in the Torah and the Gospel, but denied out of obstinacy and envy. Why, then, do you dispute about something you do not know, concerning Abraham, for which your scripture contains no statement? “God knows” the matter you dispute, “and you do not know”; rather, you are ignorant. He then expressly declares both parties’ claims false: “Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian, but a ḥanīf,” turning away from false beliefs, “one submitting to God,” obedient to his Lord’s ordinances. It does not mean that he belonged to the [later] religious community of Islam ( ملّة الإسلام ); otherwise the same objection would apply, since the religion of Islam also came later. Abraham was the imam of those who affirm the One. “He was not one of those who associate partners with Him,” as do the Jews, Christians and polytheists. This indirectly rebukes them and refutes their claim to follow his religious way. [XIV-N033] He then states who has the closest claim to him: “Those with the closest claim to Abraham”—those most particularly related and nearest to him—“are surely those who followed him,” of his community in his own time, “this Prophet,” Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, “and those who believe,” since they agree with him in most ordinances. The Prophet said: “Every prophet has protecting friends among the prophets, and my protecting friend among them is my father and my Lord’s intimate friend,” meaning Abraham, peace be upon him. “God is the protecting friend of the believers”: He supports them against the other religions and recompenses them with the utmost beneficence."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Following the prophetic way rather than claiming exclusive perfection","html":"<p>You see many who profess spiritual poverty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المتفقرة<\/bdi>) confining perfection to their own path and disputing about the paths of others. This is a tendency of the People of the Book, departing from sound guidance and correctness. The one with the closest claim to the truth is he who follows the Muhammadan Sunna, acquires praiseworthy character, renounces the two abodes, lifts his aspiration beyond the two worlds, and removes the veil of heedlessness from his heart until his Lord’s lights shine upon him. He joins those who impart prophetic spiritual training (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التربية النبوية<\/bdi>); they cast him into the seas of absolute oneness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأحدية<\/bdi>), then return him to the station of sobriety and the perfecting of others (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصحو والتكميل<\/bdi>). What an exalted station! This is the religious way of Abraham, the intimate friend, and it is what the noble Messenger brought: the beloved of the All-Merciful, the pole of the circle of time, the master of the messengers, the imam of the knowers, and the Messenger of the Lord of the worlds. God’s blessings and peace be upon him continually until the Day of Judgment.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"You see many who profess spiritual poverty ( المتفقرة ) confining perfection to their own path and disputing about the paths of others. This is a tendency of the People of the Book, departing from sound guidance and correctness. The one with the closest claim to the truth is he who follows the Muhammadan Sunna, acquires praiseworthy character, renounces the two abodes, lifts his aspiration beyond the two worlds, and removes the veil of heedlessness from his heart until his Lord’s lights shine upon him. He joins those who impart prophetic spiritual training ( التربية النبوية ); they cast him into the seas of absolute oneness ( الأحدية ), then return him to the station of sobriety and the perfecting of others ( الصحو والتكميل ). What an exalted station! This is the religious way of Abraham, the intimate friend, and it is what the noble Messenger brought: the beloved of the All-Merciful, the pole of the circle of time, the master of the messengers, the imam of the knowers, and the Messenger of the Lord of the worlds. God’s blessings and peace be upon him continually until the Day of Judgment."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then began to rebuke the Jews and recount their faults, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then began to rebuke the Jews and recount their faults, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4132,"arabic_characters":2949},{"id":"q3-69-71","q":"3:69–71","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:69–71 · Concealment, envy and the preservation of religious status","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 367–367","cairo":"I:367","source_page_first":367,"source_page_last":367,"ar":"وَدَّتْ طائِفَةٌ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ لَوْ يُضِلُّونَكُمْ وَما يُضِلُّونَ إِلاَّ أَنْفُسَهُمْ وَما يَشْعُرُونَ (69) يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَأَنْتُمْ تَشْهَدُونَ (70) يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَلْبِسُونَ الْحَقَّ بِالْباطِلِ وَتَكْتُمُونَ الْحَقَّ وَأَنْتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ (71)\nقلت: (لو) : مصدرية، أي: تمنوا إضلالكم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله لبعض المسلمين- وهم حذيفة وعمار ومعاذ- دعاهم اليهود إلى دينهم وطمعوا فيهم:\nوَدَّتْ طائِفَةٌ أي: تمنت طائفة مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ لَوْ يُضِلُّونَكُمْ أي: يفتنونكم عن دينكم، ويتلفونكم عن طريق الحق، وَما يُضِلُّونَ إِلَّا أَنْفُسَهُمْ لأن المسلمين لا يقبلون ذلك منهم، فرجع الضلال عليهم، وعاد وباله إليهم، وتضاعف عذابه عليهم، وَما يَشْعُرُونَ أن وباله راجع إليهم.\nثم صرّح الحق تعالى بعتابهم، فقال: يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ المنزلة على نبيه محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم وتجحدون رسالته؟ وَأَنْتُمْ تَشْهَدُونَ أنها من عند الله، وأنه نبيّ الله، وهو منعوت عندكم في التوراة والإنجيل، والمراد أحبارهم، أو تشهدون أنه نبيّ الله بالمعجزات الواضحات. يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَلْبِسُونَ الْحَقَّ بِالْباطِلِ بالتحريف وإبراز الباطل في صورة الحق، حتى كتمتم نعت محمد وحرفتموه، وأظهرتم موضعه الباطل الذي سولت لكم أنفسكم؟ وَتَكْتُمُونَ الْحَقَّ نبوة محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم، وَأَنْتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ أنه رسول الله حقّاً وأن دينه حق، أو:\nوأنتم عالمون بكتمانكم.\nالإشارة: ترى كثيراً من أهل الرئاسة والجاه من أولاد الصالحين، وممن ينتسب لهم، إذا رأوا من ظهر بالخصوصية في زمانهم يتمنون إضلالهم وإطفاء أنوارهم، خوفاً على زوال رئاستهم، وما يضلون إلا أنفسهم وما يشعرون، (وَاللَّهُ مُتِمُّ نُورِهِ وَلَوْ كَرِهَ الكافرون) ، وهذه نزعة يهودية سببها الحسد، والحسود لا يسود، وبعضهم يتحقق بخصوصية غيرهم، فيكتمها وهو يشهد بصحتها، فيقال لهم: لم تكفرون بآيات الله وأنتم تشهدون؟ ولم تلبسون الحق بالباطل، وتكتمون الحق وأنتم تعلمون؟.\nثم ذكر الحق- تعالى- خدع أهل الكتاب وحيلهم الفارغة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَدَّتْ طائِفَةٌ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ لَوْ يُضِلُّونَكُمْ وَما يُضِلُّونَ إِلاَّ أَنْفُسَهُمْ وَما يَشْعُرُونَ (69) يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَأَنْتُمْ تَشْهَدُونَ (70) يا أَهْلَ الْكِتابِ لِمَ تَلْبِسُونَ الْحَقَّ بِالْباطِلِ وَتَكْتُمُونَ الْحَقَّ وَأَنْتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ (71)","quran_en":"69. A group among the People of the Book would like to lead you astray, but they lead none astray except themselves, though they do not perceive it. 70. O People of the Book, why do you disbelieve in God’s signs when you yourselves bear witness? 71. O People of the Book, why do you mix truth with falsehood and conceal the truth while you know?","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Law introducing the content of a wish","html":"<p>I say: law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لو<\/bdi>) has the force of a verbal-noun clause (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدرية<\/bdi>): that is, they wished for your being led astray.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: law ( لو ) has the force of a verbal-noun clause ( مصدرية ): that is, they wished for your being led astray."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Knowing testimony and deliberate concealment","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, addresses certain Muslims—Ḥudhayfa, ʿAmmār and Muʿādh—whom the Jews had invited to their religion and hoped to win over: “A group,” that is, a party “among the People of the Book would like to lead you astray”: tempt you from your religion and ruin you by turning you from the way of truth. “But they lead none astray except themselves,” since the Muslims do not accept this from them: the misguidance recoils upon them, its evil consequence returns to them, and its punishment is multiplied against them. “They do not perceive” that its evil consequence returns to themselves.<\/p><p>The Real Most High then expressly rebukes them: “O People of the Book, why do you disbelieve in God’s signs,” sent down to His Prophet Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, and deny his mission, “when you yourselves bear witness” that they come from God and that he is God’s prophet, described in your Torah and Gospel? Those meant are their rabbis. Alternatively, you bear witness through the clear miracles that he is God’s prophet. “O People of the Book, why do you mix truth with falsehood,” by alteration and by displaying falsehood in the form of truth, concealing and changing Muḥammad’s description and putting in its place the falsehood your souls have enticed you to devise? “And conceal the truth”—Muḥammad’s prophethood—“while you know” that he truly is God’s Messenger and that his religion is true; or, while you know that you are concealing it.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, addresses certain Muslims—Ḥudhayfa, ʿAmmār and Muʿādh—whom the Jews had invited to their religion and hoped to win over: “A group,” that is, a party “among the People of the Book would like to lead you astray”: tempt you from your religion and ruin you by turning you from the way of truth. “But they lead none astray except themselves,” since the Muslims do not accept this from them: the misguidance recoils upon them, its evil consequence returns to them, and its punishment is multiplied against them. “They do not perceive” that its evil consequence returns to themselves. The Real Most High then expressly rebukes them: “O People of the Book, why do you disbelieve in God’s signs,” sent down to His Prophet Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him, and deny his mission, “when you yourselves bear witness” that they come from God and that he is God’s prophet, described in your Torah and Gospel? Those meant are their rabbis. Alternatively, you bear witness through the clear miracles that he is God’s prophet. “O People of the Book, why do you mix truth with falsehood,” by alteration and by displaying falsehood in the form of truth, concealing and changing Muḥammad’s description and putting in its place the falsehood your souls have enticed you to devise? “And conceal the truth”—Muḥammad’s prophethood—“while you know” that he truly is God’s Messenger and that his religion is true; or, while you know that you are concealing it."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Envy of living spiritual distinction","html":"<p>You see many people of leadership and standing, among the descendants of the righteous and those affiliated with them, who, when they see someone manifest special spiritual distinction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخصوصية<\/bdi>) in their own time, wish to lead him astray and extinguish his lights, fearing the loss of their own leadership. Yet they mislead only themselves and do not perceive it: “God will bring His light to completion, though the unbelievers dislike it” [61:8]. This is a Jewish tendency caused by envy; the envious man does not attain mastery. Some recognize the special distinction of others, yet conceal it while bearing witness inwardly to its truth. They are asked: Why do you disbelieve in God’s signs when you bear witness? Why do you mix truth with falsehood and conceal the truth while you know?<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"You see many people of leadership and standing, among the descendants of the righteous and those affiliated with them, who, when they see someone manifest special spiritual distinction ( الخصوصية ) in their own time, wish to lead him astray and extinguish his lights, fearing the loss of their own leadership. Yet they mislead only themselves and do not perceive it: “God will bring His light to completion, though the unbelievers dislike it” [61:8]. This is a Jewish tendency caused by envy; the envious man does not attain mastery. Some recognize the special distinction of others, yet conceal it while bearing witness inwardly to its truth. They are asked: Why do you disbelieve in God’s signs when you bear witness? Why do you mix truth with falsehood and conceal the truth while you know?"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High then mentioned the deceptions of the People of the Book and their futile stratagems, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High then mentioned the deceptions of the People of the Book and their futile stratagems, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2495,"arabic_characters":1836},{"id":"q3-72","q":"3:72","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:72 · Calculated withdrawal and the instability of spiritual commitment","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 367–368","cairo":"I:367–368","source_page_first":367,"source_page_last":368,"ar":"وَقالَتْ طائِفَةٌ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ آمِنُوا بِالَّذِي أُنْزِلَ عَلَى الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَجْهَ النَّهارِ وَاكْفُرُوا آخِرَهُ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَرْجِعُونَ (72)\nقال الحسن والسدي: تواطأ اثنا عشر رجلاً من يهود خيبر- يعني من أحبارهم- وقال بعضهم لبعض: ادخلوا في دين محمد أول النهار باللسان لا بالاعتقاد، واكفروا به آخره، وقولوا: نظرنا في كتبنا، وشاورنا علماءنا، فوجدنا محمداً ليس بذلك، وظهر لنا كذبه، وإنما نفعل ذلك حتى نشكك أصحابه. هـ. فحذَّر الله تعالى المسلمين من قولهم، فقال جلّ جلاله: وَقالَتْ طائِفَةٌ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ يعني: أحبارهم: (آمنوا بالذي أنزل على الذين آمنوا) وأظهروا الدخول في دينهم، وَجْهَ النَّهارِ وَاكْفُرُوا آخِرَهُ وقولوا: نظرنا في كتبنا، وشاورنا علماءنا، فلم نجد محمداً بالنعت الذي في التوراة، لعل أصحابه يشكون فيه- لعنهم الله وأضلّ سعيهم.\nوقيل: نزلت في شأن الكعبة، فإنَّ كعب بن الأشرف ومالك بن الصيف- من اليهود- قالا لأصحابهما: صلّوا معهم إلى الكعبة أول النهار، ثم صلّوا إلى الصخرة آخره، لعلهم يقولون: هم أعلم منا، وقد رجعوا، فيرجعون، ففضحهم الله وأبطل حيلتهم الواهية.\nالإشارة: ترى كثيراً من الناسَ يدخلون في طريق القوم، ثم تثقل عليهم أعباؤها، فيخرجون منها إما لضعفهم عن حملها، أو لكونهم دخلوا مختبرين لها، أو على حرف أو حيلة لغيرهم، فإذا رجع أحد منهم قال الناس: لو كانت صحيحة ما رجع فلان عنها، ويصدون الناس عن الدخول فيها والدوام عليها، وهذه نزعة إسرائيلية، قالوا: آمنوا وجه النهار واكفروا آخره لعلهم يرجعون وقد قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لتَسْلُكن سَنَنَ مَنْ قَبْلكم شِبراً بشبر، وذرَاعاً بِذرَاعٍ، حَتَّى لَوْ دَخلُوا جُحْر ضَبٍّ لدخلْتُموه، قالوا: اليَهُود والنًّصَارَى؟ قال: نعم، فَمنْ إذن» . وبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكر الحق- تعالى- مقالة أخرى من مقالاتهم الشنيعة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقالَتْ طائِفَةٌ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ آمِنُوا بِالَّذِي أُنْزِلَ عَلَى الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَجْهَ النَّهارِ وَاكْفُرُوا آخِرَهُ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَرْجِعُونَ (72)","quran_en":"72. A group among the People of the Book said: “Believe at the beginning of the day in what was sent down to those who believe, and reject it at its end, so that they may turn back.”","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The stratagem of professing belief and then withdrawing","html":"<p>Al-Ḥasan and al-Suddī said: twelve men among the Jews of Khaybar—that is, their rabbis—conspired together, saying to one another: “Enter Muḥammad’s religion at the beginning of the day in speech, not in conviction; reject it at the end. Say: ‘We examined our books and consulted our learned men, and found that Muḥammad is not the one described; his falsehood became clear to us.’ We shall do this only to make his companions doubt.” End of the quotation. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N034\">[XIV-N034]<\/a><\/p><p>God Most High therefore warned the Muslims against their statement. He, exalted is His majesty, said: “A group among the People of the Book”—their rabbis—“said: ‘Believe in what was sent down to those who believe,’” outwardly professing entry into their religion, “at the beginning of the day, and reject it at its end.” Say: “We examined our books and consulted our learned men, and did not find Muḥammad to have the description in the Torah,” so that his companions might come to doubt him. May God curse them and bring their endeavour to nothing.<\/p><p>Another account says that the verse was revealed concerning the Kaʿba. Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf and Mālik ibn al-Ṣayf, who were Jews, told their companions: “Pray with them towards the Kaʿba at the beginning of the day; then pray towards the Rock at its end. Perhaps they will say, ‘These people know more than we do and have turned back,’ and they too will turn back.” God exposed them and nullified their feeble stratagem.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Ḥasan and al-Suddī said: twelve men among the Jews of Khaybar—that is, their rabbis—conspired together, saying to one another: “Enter Muḥammad’s religion at the beginning of the day in speech, not in conviction; reject it at the end. Say: ‘We examined our books and consulted our learned men, and found that Muḥammad is not the one described; his falsehood became clear to us.’ We shall do this only to make his companions doubt.” End of the quotation. [XIV-N034] God Most High therefore warned the Muslims against their statement. He, exalted is His majesty, said: “A group among the People of the Book”—their rabbis—“said: ‘Believe in what was sent down to those who believe,’” outwardly professing entry into their religion, “at the beginning of the day, and reject it at its end.” Say: “We examined our books and consulted our learned men, and did not find Muḥammad to have the description in the Torah,” so that his companions might come to doubt him. May God curse them and bring their endeavour to nothing. Another account says that the verse was revealed concerning the Kaʿba. Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf and Mālik ibn al-Ṣayf, who were Jews, told their companions: “Pray with them towards the Kaʿba at the beginning of the day; then pray towards the Rock at its end. Perhaps they will say, ‘These people know more than we do and have turned back,’ and they too will turn back.” God exposed them and nullified their feeble stratagem."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Entering the path without a settled commitment","html":"<p>You see many people enter the path of the Folk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طريق القوم<\/bdi>), only to find its burdens heavy and leave it: either because they lack the strength to bear them, or because they entered merely to test it, with a precarious commitment, or as a stratagem against others. When one of them withdraws, people say: “Had it been sound, so-and-so would not have left it.” They then deter people from entering it and persevering in it. This is an Israelite tendency: “Believe at the beginning of the day and reject it at its end, so that they may turn back.” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “You will follow the ways of those before you, span for span and cubit for cubit, until, were they to enter a lizard’s hole, you would enter it.” They asked: “The Jews and Christians?” He said: “Yes; who else, then?” Success comes through God. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N035\">[XIV-N035]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"You see many people enter the path of the Folk ( طريق القوم ), only to find its burdens heavy and leave it: either because they lack the strength to bear them, or because they entered merely to test it, with a precarious commitment, or as a stratagem against others. When one of them withdraws, people say: “Had it been sound, so-and-so would not have left it.” They then deter people from entering it and persevering in it. This is an Israelite tendency: “Believe at the beginning of the day and reject it at its end, so that they may turn back.” The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “You will follow the ways of those before you, span for span and cubit for cubit, until, were they to enter a lizard’s hole, you would enter it.” They asked: “The Jews and Christians?” He said: “Yes; who else, then?” Success comes through God. [XIV-N035]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High then mentioned another of their objectionable statements, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High then mentioned another of their objectionable statements, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2373,"arabic_characters":1592}];
